WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444 align:middle line:90%
[GENTLE ORCHESTRAL MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:24.110 align:middle line:90%


00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.840 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: All right,
this is CS50's Introduction

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.190 align:middle line:90%
to Programming with Python.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:32.009 align:middle line:84%
My name is David Malan and this is
our week on functions and variables.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:35.690 align:middle line:84%
But odds are, many of you, most of you,
have never actually programmed before.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.790 align:middle line:90%
So let's start by doing just that.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.250 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead here
and open up my computer

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.220 align:middle line:84%
and on it, a program called
Visual Studio Code or VS

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.650 align:middle line:84%
Code, which is just a very
popular program nowadays

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.220 align:middle line:90%
for actually writing code.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, you don't have to write
code using this particular tool.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.590 align:middle line:84%
In fact, all we need
at the end of the day

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.742 align:middle line:84%
is a so-called text editor,
a program for writing text.

00:00:54.742 --> 00:00:57.200 align:middle line:84%
And, heck, if you really want,
you could even use something

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.180 align:middle line:90%
like Google Docs or Microsoft Word.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.952 align:middle line:84%
You'd have to save it
in the right format,

00:01:00.952 --> 00:01:04.160 align:middle line:84%
but really, at the end of the day, all
you need is a program for writing text

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.170 align:middle line:90%
because that's what code is-- text.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.090 align:middle line:84%
Now within this particular
program, I'm going

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.550 align:middle line:84%
to have the ability to
create one or more files

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.500 align:middle line:90%
via this top portion of the screen.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.810 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to do
so by diving right in

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.030 align:middle line:84%
and doing this at the
bottom of my screen.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.060 align:middle line:84%
At the bottom of my screen is
a so-called terminal window

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:25.310 align:middle line:84%
and this is a command line interface
or CLI interface to the underlying

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.340 align:middle line:84%
computer, be it your Mac or your PC
or even some server in the cloud.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.240 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm going to do here is
literally write, "code" and then

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.530 align:middle line:84%
the name of the file that I want
to code, for instance, Hello.py.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.310 align:middle line:84%
As we'll soon see, any program that you
write in Python generally has a file

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.210 align:middle line:84%
name that ends in .py to indicate to
the computer that it's indeed a program

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.320 align:middle line:90%
written in Python.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.440 align:middle line:84%
Now you'll see here at the top of
my screen, I have a blinking cursor,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.800 align:middle line:84%
a line one-- which is where the very
first line of my code is going to go--

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.730 align:middle line:84%
and then just a tab that reminds me
of the name of this file Hello.py.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.370 align:middle line:84%
And without even
knowing much Python, I'm

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.770 align:middle line:84%
going to write my very first
program here as follows--

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:02.120 align:middle line:90%
print("hello,world").

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:08.396 align:middle line:90%


00:02:08.396 --> 00:02:10.729 align:middle line:84%
And you'll see that, at my
keyboard, some of my thoughts

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:11.604 align:middle line:90%
were finished for me.

00:02:11.604 --> 00:02:14.870 align:middle line:84%
I only had to type one parenthesis and
the other one automatically appeared,

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.590 align:middle line:84%
and that's just the feature that we'll
see of tools like this tool here.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.080 align:middle line:84%
Now even if you've
never programmed before,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.610 align:middle line:84%
odds are you can guess, infer what
this simple program is going to do.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.250 align:middle line:84%
And it's only one line.
print("hello,world").

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.030 align:middle line:90%


00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.830 align:middle line:84%
Indeed, when I run this
program ultimately,

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.948 align:middle line:84%
it's just going to say,
Hello to the world.

00:02:35.948 --> 00:02:37.490 align:middle line:90%
And, in fact, this is a very famous--

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.440 align:middle line:84%
perhaps the most
canonical-- program that you

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.530 align:middle line:84%
can write as your very first program
in Python or any other language,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.280 align:middle line:90%
and so that's what I've done here.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.230 align:middle line:84%
But on my Mac, my PC, even my
phone, I'm generally in the habit,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.170 align:middle line:84%
like you, of running programs
by double clicking an icon

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.760 align:middle line:90%
or just tapping on the screen.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.920 align:middle line:90%
But I see no such icons here.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.730 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, that's because my
interface to at least my current Mac

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.180 align:middle line:84%
or PC or some server in the
cloud, is again only a CLI--

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.340 align:middle line:84%
command line interface--
which even though it

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.820 align:middle line:84%
might feel like it's a step back
from the menus and buttons and icons

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.730 align:middle line:84%
that you and I take for granted
every day, you'll find, we think,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.670 align:middle line:84%
that it's ultimately a much more
powerful interface and incredibly

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.920 align:middle line:84%
popular to use among
programmers in the real world.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.245 align:middle line:84%
So to run this program, I'm
going to have to use a command

00:03:20.245 --> 00:03:23.120 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to move my cursor back
down to the bottom of the screen

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.790 align:middle line:84%
here where previously I
already ran one command--

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:30.410 align:middle line:84%
the command Code which has the effect
of opening VS Code in my computer,

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.560 align:middle line:84%
and then I passed in the name of
the file that I wanted to code up.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.000 align:middle line:84%
Now I have a chance to
type a second command,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.090 align:middle line:84%
and you'll see I see
a second dollar sign.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.360 align:middle line:84%
Now the dollar sign here doesn't
indicate any kind of currency or money,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.340 align:middle line:84%
it just is the symbol
that's generally used

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.610 align:middle line:84%
to indicate your prompt-- where
the command line interface wants

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:47.990 align:middle line:90%
you to put those commands.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.530 align:middle line:84%
Now the command I can run
here is going to be this--

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.310 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to run Python of Hello.py.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.490 align:middle line:90%
Now why is that?

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.380 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that when I actually
write code in a language like Python,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.120 align:middle line:90%
it's of course stored in that file--

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.063 align:middle line:84%
Hello.py, but I need to
interpret the code top to bottom,

00:04:07.063 --> 00:04:09.230 align:middle line:84%
left to right so that the
computer knows what to do.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.940 align:middle line:84%
Indeed, at the end of the
day, even if you don't really

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.920 align:middle line:84%
know much about
computers, you've probably

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:16.790 align:middle line:84%
heard the computers only understand
zeros and ones, the so-called binary

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:17.390 align:middle line:90%
system.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:21.769 align:middle line:84%
Well if that's the case, then something
that says "print" and parentheses

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:25.430 align:middle line:84%
and quote unquote hello world,
is not surely zeros and ones.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.750 align:middle line:84%
We have to somehow translate
it into the zeros and ones

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.000 align:middle line:90%
that the computer understands.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.360 align:middle line:84%
Now fortunately, so long as you've
installed such a program in advance,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.380 align:middle line:84%
there's a program as well
as a language called Python.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.390 align:middle line:84%
So Python is not only a language
in which we're going to write code,

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:42.590 align:middle line:84%
it's also a program otherwise
known as an interpreter

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:46.490 align:middle line:84%
that you install for free on your own
Mac or PC or some server in the cloud

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.990 align:middle line:84%
and you can then run that program, that
interpreter, passing to it as input

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:53.870 align:middle line:84%
the name of your file
like mine here, Hello.py.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.000 align:middle line:84%
And then that program,
that interpreter, will

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.210 align:middle line:84%
handle the process of reading
it top to bottom, left to right

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:02.210 align:middle line:84%
and translating it effectively
into those zeros and ones

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.170 align:middle line:90%
that the computer can understand.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.360 align:middle line:90%
So let's do just that.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.190 align:middle line:90%
Let me go back to VS Code here.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:11.030 align:middle line:84%
I already typed out Python of
Hello.py but I didn't get hit Enter.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.430 align:middle line:84%
And that's what's now going
to kick off this command

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.190 align:middle line:84%
and hopefully, if I didn't
mess any of this up,

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:20.370 align:middle line:84%
I should see my very first
program's output to the screen.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.480 align:middle line:90%
And voila, hello, world.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.970 align:middle line:84%
So if you too have typed
exactly that same code

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.400 align:middle line:84%
and have executed exactly
that same command,

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.840 align:middle line:84%
you will have written your very
first program in this case in Python.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.790 align:middle line:84%
Well now let's take a
step back and consider,

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.190 align:middle line:84%
what is it that we
actually just did and what

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.930 align:middle line:90%
is it we're looking here on the screen?

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.450 align:middle line:84%
Well first and foremost, in
most any programming language,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.950 align:middle line:84%
you tend to have access to
what are called functions.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.460 align:middle line:84%
A function is like an
action or a verb that

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.800 align:middle line:90%
lets you do something in the program.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.730 align:middle line:84%
And generally speaking, any language
comes with some predetermined set

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:57.097 align:middle line:84%
of functions-- some very basic actions
or verbs that the computer will already

00:05:57.097 --> 00:05:59.180 align:middle line:84%
know how to do for you,
that the language, really,

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:00.530 align:middle line:90%
will know how to do for you.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:04.640 align:middle line:84%
And you, the programmer, the human,
can use those functions at will

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.020 align:middle line:90%
to get the computer to do those things.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.835 align:middle line:84%
Now the program in question here,
Hello.py is using one function.

00:06:11.835 --> 00:06:13.460 align:middle line:90%
And you could perhaps guess what it is.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.760 align:middle line:84%
That function is, of course,
going to be this function print

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.430 align:middle line:84%
and that print function,
of course, doesn't

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.340 align:middle line:90%
print some preordained string of text.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:26.240 align:middle line:84%
That is to say, it prints whatever
it is you want it to print.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.600 align:middle line:84%
And here too, do we have another
piece of terminology in the world

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.430 align:middle line:90%
of programming, namely arguments.

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:36.830 align:middle line:84%
An argument is an input to a function
that somehow influences its behavior.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.660 align:middle line:84%
The people who invented
Python, of course,

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.790 align:middle line:84%
didn't necessarily know
what it is you and I

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:43.850 align:middle line:84%
are going to want to print to the
screen, so they designed this print

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:46.580 align:middle line:84%
function using these
parentheses with the ability

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.340 align:middle line:84%
to take as input some
string of text, be it

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.250 align:middle line:84%
in English or any other
human language, that is

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:56.540 align:middle line:84%
what you want this function
ultimately to print onto the screen.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.640 align:middle line:84%
And what is it that the program
is ultimately doing on the screen?

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.070 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's printing, of course.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.230 align:middle line:84%
It's showing us hello,
world on the screen.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.743 align:middle line:84%
And that's generally in
programming known as a side effect.

00:07:05.743 --> 00:07:07.160 align:middle line:90%
It can be visual, it can be audio.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.470 align:middle line:84%
In this case, it's something
that appears on the screen

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.690 align:middle line:84%
and functions, therefore, can
indeed have these side effects.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.650 align:middle line:84%
One of the things they can
do as this verb or action is

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:19.100 align:middle line:84%
to display on the screen as a side
effect, something like those words

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:20.240 align:middle line:90%
that we wanted--

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:21.870 align:middle line:90%
hello, world.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.080 align:middle line:84%
So that's my first program and,
you know, I'm feeling pretty good.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.570 align:middle line:90%
Everything worked as planned.

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:28.490 align:middle line:84%
I didn't make any
mistakes, but, honestly,

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.560 align:middle line:84%
when you're learning
how to program and even

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.250 align:middle line:84%
once you've learned how to program years
later, you're going to make mistakes.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.615 align:middle line:84%
And those mistakes, of
course, are referred

00:07:36.615 --> 00:07:38.990 align:middle line:84%
to a term you might already
know, which is that of a bug.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.230 align:middle line:84%
A bug is a mistake in a program
and they can take so many forms.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:46.460 align:middle line:84%
And take comfort, perhaps, in
knowing that over the coming weeks,

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.450 align:middle line:90%
you're going to make so many mistakes.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.320 align:middle line:84%
You're going to have so many bugs
in your code, just like I did

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.490 align:middle line:90%
and just as I still do.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.610 align:middle line:84%
And those bugs themselves
are just mistakes

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.230 align:middle line:90%
that are problems for you to solve.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.540 align:middle line:84%
And over the weeks to come,
we're going to give you

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:03.760 align:middle line:84%
a lot of tools both mental and technical
via which you can solve those problems.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.010 align:middle line:84%
But just don't get discouraged if when
writing your program for the first time

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.630 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't even work that first time,
it will with time, with practice,

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:12.810 align:middle line:90%
and with experience.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:15.200 align:middle line:84%
So let me deliberately
now make a mistake

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.710 align:middle line:84%
that there was a non-zero chance I
might have done accidentally already,

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:19.760 align:middle line:90%
but I got lucky.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.280 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and
just suppose I forgot

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:24.950 align:middle line:84%
to include something like
the closing parenthesis

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:26.570 align:middle line:90%
at the end of this line of code.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:30.451 align:middle line:84%
The code is almost correct,
it's like 99% of the way there,

00:08:30.451 --> 00:08:32.659 align:middle line:84%
but now that I've pointed
it out, it's pretty obvious

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:34.820 align:middle line:84%
that it's missing that
closed parenthesis.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:39.530 align:middle line:84%
But even little, seemingly minor details
like that, that you and I as humans

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:40.740 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't really care about.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.300 align:middle line:84%
And if you're sending an email or a text
message, whatever, it's just a typo.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.605 align:middle line:90%
It's not that big a deal.

00:08:45.605 --> 00:08:47.480 align:middle line:84%
It is going to be a big
deal to the computer.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:49.230 align:middle line:84%
A computer is going
to take you literally,

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.210 align:middle line:84%
and if you don't finish your thought
in the way the language expects,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.380 align:middle line:84%
it's not going to
necessarily run at all.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.112 align:middle line:90%
So let's do this.

00:08:56.112 --> 00:08:58.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go ahead here and
clear my screen down at the bottom

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:01.340 align:middle line:84%
just so I can start fresh, and
I'm going to go ahead and run

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:05.420 align:middle line:84%
this version of my program after
having made that change by deleting

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:06.410 align:middle line:90%
the parenthesis.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:10.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go ahead and type Python
again of Hello.py and this time

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.040 align:middle line:84%
when I hit Enter, I'm hoping
I'm going to see hello, world,

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.850 align:middle line:84%
but here we have an error on
the screen, a so-called syntax

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:21.140 align:middle line:84%
error, which refers to my having
made a mistake at my keyboard.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.030 align:middle line:84%
And this one, fortunately,
is pretty straightforward.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:28.410 align:middle line:84%
It indeed says that this open
parenthesis was never closed.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.945 align:middle line:90%
And so that's probably pretty intuitive.

00:09:30.945 --> 00:09:33.320 align:middle line:84%
Now what I need to do, I need
to, of course, to close it.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.530 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, sometimes the error
messages we'll see in the coming weeks

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:38.840 align:middle line:84%
are not going to be
nearly that user-friendly.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.720 align:middle line:84%
But there too again, with
experience, with practice, will you

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:44.360 align:middle line:90%
get better at debugging such programs.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:46.650 align:middle line:84%
Let me now make sure that I
indeed fixed it correctly.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.420 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and run now
Hello.py and hit Enter and, voila,

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:51.890 align:middle line:90%
we're back in business.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.430 align:middle line:84%
Well let me pause here and see
if we have any questions now

00:09:55.430 --> 00:10:02.480 align:middle line:84%
about Python itself, writing, or running
even the simplest of these programs.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could I write code inside a
word or, for example, Microsoft Excel?

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.473 align:middle line:90%
And what's the barrier to doing that?

00:10:09.473 --> 00:10:11.390 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: A really
good question, and allow

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:14.630 align:middle line:84%
me to very explicitly say
to the entire internet

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.810 align:middle line:84%
that you should not write
code with Microsoft Word.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:20.480 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned that only
because it's a tool via which

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.283 align:middle line:84%
you can write text and code is,
at the end of the day, just text.

00:10:23.283 --> 00:10:24.950 align:middle line:90%
But it's not the right tool for the job.

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.890 align:middle line:84%
We don't need bold facing,
underlining, paragraphs and the like.

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:31.580 align:middle line:84%
We generally want something much simpler
than Microsoft Word or Google Docs.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:36.110 align:middle line:84%
And so VS Code is an example of just
a more general purpose text editor.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:39.110 align:middle line:84%
Its purpose in life is to allow
you, the human, to edit text.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:42.050 align:middle line:84%
Nowadays these text editors
come with many more features.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:44.600 align:middle line:84%
In fact, you'll notice
that even in my code here,

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.570 align:middle line:84%
even though it's just one line,
there's a bit of color to it.

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.910 align:middle line:84%
The word "print" for me
is appearing in blue.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:51.050 align:middle line:90%
The parentheses are black.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:54.290 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see as we write more lines
of code, more and more of the lines

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.630 align:middle line:90%
will come to life in various colors.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:58.993 align:middle line:84%
Now that's just one
feature of a text editor.

00:10:58.993 --> 00:11:01.910 align:middle line:84%
We'll see too that it has features
like this built-in terminal window.

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:05.120 align:middle line:84%
It's going to have a built-in tool
for debugging or finding problems

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:05.780 align:middle line:90%
with code.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.760 align:middle line:84%
And it's just a very
popular tool nowadays,

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.560 align:middle line:84%
but there are many,
many others out there.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.900 align:middle line:84%
You're welcome to use them
for this course and beyond.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.120 align:middle line:84%
We just happen to use
this one in large part

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:18.980 align:middle line:84%
too because you can also use VS
Code nowadays for free in the cloud.

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:22.190 align:middle line:84%
How about one other question
here on programming with Python

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:24.950 align:middle line:84%
or hello, world or
syntax more generally?

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:28.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah I was
trying to ask if it's not

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:31.923 align:middle line:84%
possible to run the computer
using the terminal window?

00:11:31.923 --> 00:11:33.590 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: I think I heard is it not--

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:37.530 align:middle line:84%
if it's possible to run the program
without the terminal window?

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:38.030 align:middle line:90%
Are you--

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:38.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes, sir.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:39.870 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: OK, you froze for me again.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:41.730 align:middle line:90%
But let me infer what the question is.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.660 align:middle line:84%
So in this environment, as
I've configured my computer,

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:48.320 align:middle line:84%
I can only run these Python
programs via the terminal window.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.140 align:middle line:84%
Now that's good for me, the
programmer, or the person who's

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:53.390 align:middle line:84%
trying to learn how to
program, but it's not very good

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:57.050 align:middle line:84%
if you want to ship this software and
have other people use your actual code.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:01.550 align:middle line:84%
You can absolutely write programs
and then allow other people to use,

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:05.300 align:middle line:84%
not a command line interface, but
a graphical user interface or GUI--

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:08.390 align:middle line:84%
G-U-I. This is just one
mechanism and perhaps, I think,

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.700 align:middle line:84%
the best one with which
to start writing code

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:14.045 align:middle line:84%
because eventually it's going
to give us a lot more control.

00:12:14.045 --> 00:12:15.920 align:middle line:84%
Allow me to forge ahead
here, but please feel

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.820 align:middle line:84%
free to continue asking questions
along the way if only via the chat.

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:23.690 align:middle line:84%
Let's consider now how we might
go about improving this program.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:27.530 align:middle line:84%
Let's go about improving this program
to make it a little more interactive

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:30.740 align:middle line:84%
and not just assume that everyone
is going to want to be greeted

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:32.120 align:middle line:90%
more generically as hello, world.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:35.120 align:middle line:84%
Let's see if I can't get this program
to say something like Hello, David

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:40.280 align:middle line:84%
or Hello, Jeremiah or Hello, Horatio
or whatever the actual user's name is.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:43.670 align:middle line:84%
Well to do this I'm going
to go back up to Hello to pi

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:46.950 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to add another line of
code at the very top that simply says,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:52.490 align:middle line:84%
for instance, what's your name, quote
unquote with an extra space at the end.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.530 align:middle line:84%
So I'm printing to the user asking
them a question for some input,

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.940 align:middle line:84%
but now I need another function to
actually get input from the user.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:02.550 align:middle line:84%
And, perfectly, enough Python
comes with a function named input.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:06.470 align:middle line:84%
So here I'm going to go ahead and
call a function input, open paren,

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:07.400 align:middle line:90%
close paren.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:11.000 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to prompt the user
with just a blinking cursor waiting

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.440 align:middle line:90%
for them to type something in.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:15.860 align:middle line:84%
Now it turns out, if I read the
documentation for the input function,

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.210 align:middle line:90%
it actually takes an argument itself.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.740 align:middle line:84%
I don't need to use "print" separately
and then prompt the user for input.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:25.100 align:middle line:84%
So I can actually simplify this
code before we even use it.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.430 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go ahead here and
take that same string from print,

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.800 align:middle line:84%
put it as an argument
to the input function,

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.383 align:middle line:90%
and get rid of the "print" altogether.

00:13:32.383 --> 00:13:34.967 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, that "print" would
have added a new line anyway.

00:13:34.967 --> 00:13:37.340 align:middle line:84%
So now I've just got a prompt
where the user's cursor is

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:41.030 align:middle line:84%
going to end up blinking at the end of
the line asking them, what's your name?

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:46.580 align:middle line:84%
In my terminal window I'm going
to run Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:47.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:48.150 align:middle line:90%
We're making progress.

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:51.280 align:middle line:84%
It seems that this new function input
is indeed prompting me, the human,

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:51.780 align:middle line:90%
for input.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.155 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to type in my name--

00:13:53.155 --> 00:13:54.290 align:middle line:90%
David-- and hit Enter.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:57.440 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, it doesn't
really do anything with my name,

00:13:57.440 --> 00:13:59.240 align:middle line:90%
it just outputs it immediately.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:01.160 align:middle line:84%
All right, well, I
could fix this, right?

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:05.480 align:middle line:84%
I could go up to line 2 and I
could change "world" to "David,"

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:10.310 align:middle line:84%
and then back in my terminal window
here I can do Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:11.150 align:middle line:90%
What's your name?

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:12.410 align:middle line:90%
David, Enter.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:13.105 align:middle line:90%
And there we go.

00:14:13.105 --> 00:14:14.480 align:middle line:90%
All right now I'm up and running.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:17.520 align:middle line:90%
Now my program is working as intended.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:21.410 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this isn't really
working as intended here.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:25.070 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and try pretending
to be my colleague, Carter here.

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:26.760 align:middle line:90%
Well Carter's name is this.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.927 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go ahead and hit Enter and
I'll see, of course, Hello, Carter--

00:14:29.927 --> 00:14:32.520 align:middle line:84%
well, obviously not, because
I've hardcoded, so to speak,

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:35.010 align:middle line:84%
I've written literally my
name inside of the string.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:40.040 align:middle line:84%
So we need some way now of actually
getting back what the user's input is

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:41.780 align:middle line:90%
and doing something with it ultimately.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.930 align:middle line:84%
And for this we're going to leverage
another feature of programming,

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:47.510 align:middle line:84%
specifically a feature of some
functions, which is that they

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:49.850 align:middle line:90%
can have return values as well.

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:52.700 align:middle line:84%
If you think of input as
being, again, this action,

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.880 align:middle line:84%
this verb-- you can actually
personify it as maybe a person,

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.130 align:middle line:84%
like a friend of yours that
you've asked a question of

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:00.797 align:middle line:84%
and you've asked your friend to
go get input from someone else--

00:15:00.797 --> 00:15:02.210 align:middle line:90%
go ask that person their name.

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:05.630 align:middle line:84%
And if your friend comes back
knowing that person's name, well,

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:08.357 align:middle line:84%
wouldn't it be nice if they
handed that name back to you?

00:15:08.357 --> 00:15:10.940 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of what we need
metaphorically the function to do,

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:15.200 align:middle line:84%
is get the user's input and then hand
it back to me so that I, the programmer,

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:16.610 align:middle line:90%
can do something with it.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.280 align:middle line:84%
But if it's going to
be handed back to me,

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:23.060 align:middle line:84%
I kind of want to put it somewhere
so that I can then print it back

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:23.720 align:middle line:90%
on the screen.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:26.970 align:middle line:84%
I need to do the equivalent of take out
like a piece of paper or Post-It note,

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:31.040 align:middle line:84%
write down on this piece of paper
what it is the human has said,

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:34.650 align:middle line:84%
so that I can then feed it into
as input that print function.

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:38.120 align:middle line:84%
And to do that, we're going to need
one more feature of programming, namely

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:38.960 align:middle line:90%
variables.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:42.410 align:middle line:84%
And odds are, most everyone's familiar
with variables from math class

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:45.380 align:middle line:84%
way back when-- x and
y and z and the like.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:48.110 align:middle line:84%
Well, programming has that
same capability, this ability

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:51.560 align:middle line:84%
to create a variable-- in this
case, in the computer's memory--

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.100 align:middle line:90%
not just on a piece of paper.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.620 align:middle line:90%
And that variable can store a value--

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:58.910 align:middle line:84%
a number, some text, even
an image or video or more.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:06.740 align:middle line:84%
A variable is just a container for
some value inside of a computer

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:08.300 align:middle line:90%
or inside of your own program.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.303 align:middle line:84%
So how do I go about
expressing myself in this way?

00:16:11.303 --> 00:16:13.220 align:middle line:84%
Well I think what I'm
going to do is introduce

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:16.730 align:middle line:84%
a variable that's a little more
interestingly named than x or y.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:19.970 align:middle line:90%
I could just say this-- x = input.

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:23.780 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to use a better name than
a typical mathematical variable here

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:26.240 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to literally
call my variable "name," why?

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:29.073 align:middle line:84%
Well in programming, because I have
a whole keyboard in front of me,

00:16:29.073 --> 00:16:32.150 align:middle line:84%
I can use more descriptive terms
to describe what it is I'm writing.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:36.590 align:middle line:84%
And now, though, there's an opportunity
to consider a specific piece of syntax.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:39.470 align:middle line:84%
We've seen parentheses, we've
seen quotes, all of which

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:42.020 align:middle line:84%
are necessary when passing
inputs to a function,

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:47.810 align:middle line:84%
but this equal sign here that's in
between input on the right and name

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:49.820 align:middle line:90%
on the left is actually important.

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:52.490 align:middle line:84%
And it's technically not
an equal sign per se.

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:56.060 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean equality as
much as it means assignment.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.000 align:middle line:84%
So in Python and many
programming languages,

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.610 align:middle line:84%
a single equal sign is
the assignment operator

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:03.860 align:middle line:84%
and what that means
specifically is that, you

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:08.839 align:middle line:84%
want to assign from right to left
whatever the user's input is.

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:14.540 align:middle line:84%
So the equal sign copies from the
right to the left whatever the return

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:17.420 align:middle line:90%
value of the function on the right is.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:20.670 align:middle line:84%
So again, the input function
clearly gets input from the user.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:24.140 align:middle line:84%
That's why I was able to type
my name or Carter's, but it also

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:29.390 align:middle line:84%
sort of behind the scenes hands that
value, that return value, back to me

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:30.230 align:middle line:90%
the programmer.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.680 align:middle line:84%
And if I use an equal sign and a
variable, no matter what I call it,

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:38.460 align:middle line:84%
I can store that input in that
variable so as to reuse it later.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:40.670 align:middle line:84%
So now, sitting in the
computer's memory somewhere,

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:44.870 align:middle line:84%
is a container containing "David"
quote, unquote or "Carter" quote,

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:47.490 align:middle line:84%
unquote, or whatever
the human has typed in.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.280 align:middle line:90%
But here it's easy to make a mistake.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:53.850 align:middle line:84%
Suppose I decide to try
to print that name and so

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:57.800 align:middle line:84%
I kind of on a hunch type
in this, hello, name.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:00.440 align:middle line:84%
Just kind of plugging in
the name of the variable.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:05.300 align:middle line:84%
Well let me go ahead here and run
Python of Hello.py and hit Enter.

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:07.040 align:middle line:90%
That's going to prompt me for my name.

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:08.207 align:middle line:90%
And let me type in my name--

00:18:08.207 --> 00:18:10.440 align:middle line:90%
D-A-V-I-D. But I haven't hit Enter yet.

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:15.350 align:middle line:84%
And perhaps via the chat, what's going
to happen here when I now hit Enter?

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:17.840 align:middle line:90%
I'm hoping it says, Hello, David.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:22.580 align:middle line:84%
I'd be OK if it says Hello, world, but I
don't want it to say what it's actually

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:25.370 align:middle line:84%
going to say and, yep, what
we're seeing in the chat is,

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:28.640 align:middle line:84%
well, it's probably going to
say literally Hello, name.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:30.030 align:middle line:90%
So that's not quite right.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:34.250 align:middle line:84%
So we need another way of printing
out the value inside of that variable

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.785 align:middle line:90%
rather than just this word "name."

00:18:36.785 --> 00:18:38.910 align:middle line:84%
Well let me try this in a
couple of different ways.

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:40.890 align:middle line:90%
Let me try this as follows--

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:44.390 align:middle line:84%
let me go ahead and maybe undo this
because I've gotten pretty good already

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:45.550 align:middle line:90%
at saying Hello.

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:48.050 align:middle line:84%
So let's draw that line in the
sand and just say, all right,

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:50.360 align:middle line:84%
let's get at least get
Hello comma out the door.

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:53.790 align:middle line:84%
Let's now print name and just on
a hunch, I'm going to try this.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.115 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to use print again--

00:18:55.115 --> 00:18:57.740 align:middle line:84%
because you can use these functions
as many times as you need--

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:02.660 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to pass to the print
function the variable called name.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:04.730 align:middle line:84%
But notice I'm being
a little clever now.

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:07.970 align:middle line:84%
I'm not putting it in double quotes
because we've seen already that double

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:10.610 align:middle line:84%
quotes means literally
print out N-A-M-E.

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:13.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting rid of the quotes
this time in hopes that,

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:19.440 align:middle line:84%
now by passing the variable called name
to the function called print it will,

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:23.480 align:middle line:84%
in fact, go about printing the
contents of that variable that is,

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:24.808 align:middle line:90%
its so-called value.

00:19:24.808 --> 00:19:26.600 align:middle line:84%
All right, let's go
ahead and do this here.

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.030 align:middle line:90%
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:30.020 align:middle line:90%
What's your name?

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:30.590 align:middle line:90%
David.

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:35.520 align:middle line:84%
And now, crossing my fingers
still, I see Hello comma David.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:36.020 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.090 align:middle line:90%
So it's not the best program.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.670 align:middle line:84%
I'm kind of cutting some
corners here, so to speak.

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:44.128 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying Hello, David
on two separate lines.

00:19:44.128 --> 00:19:45.920 align:middle line:84%
So it's not as elegant,
it's not as pretty,

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.950 align:middle line:84%
it's not as grammatically appropriate
in English as just saying it

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:52.820 align:middle line:84%
all in one breath on one line, but at
least I've solved the problem, just not

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:54.020 align:middle line:90%
very well yet.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.390 align:middle line:84%
But let me take a step back
now and perhaps introduce

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:59.070 align:middle line:84%
a couple of other concepts with
which we should be familiar,

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:03.260 align:middle line:84%
which is as our programs get longer and
they're no longer just one line or two

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:07.370 align:middle line:84%
or even three, eventually our programs
are going to become dozens of lines,

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.020 align:middle line:90%
maybe even hundreds of lines long.

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:11.990 align:middle line:84%
Let's set the stage for
success moving forward.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:14.840 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that Python and
a lot of programming languages

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:17.120 align:middle line:90%
also support something called comments.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:21.200 align:middle line:84%
Comments are notes to
yourself in your code

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:24.260 align:middle line:84%
and you include comments by way
of a special symbol-- in Python

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.150 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be the
hash symbol, typically--

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.980 align:middle line:84%
and that allows you to
write the equivalent

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.950 align:middle line:84%
of a note to yourself but in a way
that's not going to break your code.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:33.380 align:middle line:84%
The computer actually
ignores your comment.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:36.030 align:middle line:84%
It's just there for you, it's
just there for your teacher,

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.030 align:middle line:84%
it's just there for
your colleague with whom

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:40.020 align:middle line:90%
you're sharing ultimately that code.

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:42.320 align:middle line:84%
So if I go back to VS
Code here and I just

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.040 align:middle line:84%
want to add some comments to this
program to explain to my teacher,

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:49.325 align:middle line:84%
to myself, to my colleagues
what this program is doing,

00:20:49.325 --> 00:20:50.700 align:middle line:90%
well, let's go ahead and do that.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:54.020 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go at the very
top of my program and on line 1

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:56.990 align:middle line:84%
now I'm going to move that
original line of code down a bit,

00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:00.530 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to add a hash, and I'm
going to say something like this,

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:03.210 align:middle line:90%
ask user for their name.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:05.040 align:middle line:90%
Now, I don't have to use that language.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.950 align:middle line:90%
I don't have to use that text.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:08.910 align:middle line:84%
I could use any human
language whatsoever.

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:10.250 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't have to be English.

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:14.120 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to now, below that,
just say something like this--

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.012 align:middle line:90%
say Hello to user.

00:21:16.012 --> 00:21:17.720 align:middle line:84%
And you'll notice that
VS Code by default

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:19.302 align:middle line:90%
is kind of graying out my comments.

00:21:19.302 --> 00:21:22.010 align:middle line:84%
They're no longer blue, there's
no red, there's no color in them.

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:24.240 align:middle line:84%
And that's just because
they're notes to myself

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.730 align:middle line:84%
and the computer ultimately
is going to ignore them.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.350 align:middle line:84%
But what we have now is
two comments-- ask user

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:32.380 align:middle line:84%
for their name and then a second
comment, say Hello to user.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:35.590 align:middle line:84%
And I've just kind of
commented each chunk of code,

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:40.180 align:middle line:84%
like each line or lines plural of code,
that are doing something noteworthy.

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:40.750 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:43.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, tomorrow morning
when I wake up having

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:47.740 align:middle line:84%
slept for quite some time, forgotten
what it is I did the previous day,

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:49.840 align:middle line:84%
it's convenient with
comments to just see

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.510 align:middle line:84%
in English or your own
human language what

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:55.810 align:middle line:84%
it is this program is doing so that
you don't have to read the code itself

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:59.200 align:middle line:84%
and, better yet, if there's
maybe a mistake down the road,

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:02.740 align:middle line:84%
you can read what your intention was
and then you can look at the code

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:06.050 align:middle line:84%
and figure out if your code is
now doing what you intended.

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:08.920 align:middle line:84%
So this isn't really necessary
for a program this small.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.287 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty obvious with just
one or two or three lines

00:22:11.287 --> 00:22:12.370 align:middle line:90%
what the program is doing.

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:14.668 align:middle line:84%
It's just as fast to read
the code than the comments,

00:22:14.668 --> 00:22:16.960 align:middle line:84%
but getting into this habit
is generally a good thing--

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:21.220 align:middle line:84%
to comment your code every one or a
few lines so as to remind yourself

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:24.250 align:middle line:84%
and others what it is your
intent and your code is doing.

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:26.590 align:middle line:90%
What's nice about comments too is this--

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:30.940 align:middle line:84%
comments can also serve to be
sort of a to-do list for yourself.

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:33.850 align:middle line:84%
There's this notion in
programming of pseudocode.

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:35.350 align:middle line:90%
Pseudocode isn't a formal thing.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:37.300 align:middle line:90%
It's not one specific language.

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:40.090 align:middle line:84%
It's just using English
or your own human language

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:44.590 align:middle line:84%
to express your thoughts succinctly,
methodically, algorithmically,

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:45.460 align:middle line:90%
so to speak.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:48.070 align:middle line:84%
But pseudocode, therefore,
because it's not Python

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:51.130 align:middle line:84%
and it's not necessarily
English, it just kind of allows

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:53.520 align:middle line:84%
you to outline your
program even in advance.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:56.532 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, if I
wasn't sure today how

00:22:56.532 --> 00:22:58.240 align:middle line:84%
I wanted to go about
writing this program

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:01.720 align:middle line:84%
but I didn't know what I wanted
to do, I could have started today

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:04.570 align:middle line:90%
by just writing this in Hello.py.

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:07.870 align:middle line:84%
No code, I could have written just
a couple of comments to myself--

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:09.460 align:middle line:90%
Step 1, ask user for their name.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:11.440 align:middle line:90%
Step 2, say Hello to user.

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:15.520 align:middle line:84%
Then once I've outlined my program
in pseudocode, then I can go in there

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:18.190 align:middle line:84%
and say, all right, how do I
ask the user for their name?

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:21.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, I can do input
"what's your name" question?

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:26.080 align:middle line:84%
And then on the left here, I can maybe
put a variable and assign it to that.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.730 align:middle line:90%
OK, how do I say Hello to the user?

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:30.280 align:middle line:84%
Well, I know I can use print
to say things on the screen.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:32.920 align:middle line:90%
Let me say hello, and let me--

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.210 align:middle line:90%
OK, let me now print the person's name.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:38.960 align:middle line:84%
So again, pseudocode is a nice way
of structuring your to-do list,

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:41.680 align:middle line:84%
especially if you have no
idea how to write the code,

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:45.993 align:middle line:84%
because it breaks a bigger program
down into small bite-sized tasks.

00:23:45.993 --> 00:23:48.160 align:middle line:84%
All right, let me pause
here to see if there are now

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:56.390 align:middle line:84%
any questions on comments, pseudocode,
return values, or variables.

00:23:56.390 --> 00:23:59.170 align:middle line:90%
Any questions we can clear up here?

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:02.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, my question
is does the function input

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:06.843 align:middle line:84%
work for any type of
information, or only for words?

00:24:06.843 --> 00:24:08.510 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, really good question.

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:12.070 align:middle line:84%
So according to its documentation, and
we'll look more at formal documentation

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.290 align:middle line:84%
soon, input is going
to expect what's called

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:16.900 align:middle line:84%
a string, that is a
sequence of text, be it

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:19.030 align:middle line:90%
in English or any other human language.

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:23.660 align:middle line:84%
But it's indeed going to be expecting
text with which to prompt the user.

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:24.340 align:middle line:90%
A good question.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:26.632 align:middle line:84%
How about another question
from the group, if we could?

00:24:26.632 --> 00:24:30.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I wanted to ask how would
I make a several line comment?

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:32.770 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: Oh, how do you
do many lines of comments,

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:34.000 align:middle line:90%
if I'm hearing you correctly?

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:34.330 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:35.290 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: Sure.

00:24:35.290 --> 00:24:38.380 align:middle line:84%
You would just keep
doing them like this.

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:43.300 align:middle line:84%
You just prefix each of the lines with
a hash symbol, like I'm doing here.

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:46.990 align:middle line:84%
There is another technique for doing
multi-line comments in Python that

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:48.670 align:middle line:90%
actually tend to have special meaning.

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:53.650 align:middle line:84%
You can do three double quotes like
this and then anything in between here

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:54.910 align:middle line:90%
is a comment.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:55.960 align:middle line:90%
That's another technique.

00:24:55.960 --> 00:24:57.502 align:middle line:90%
Or you can use single quotes as well.

00:24:57.502 --> 00:25:00.257 align:middle line:84%
But more on those, I
think, another time.

00:25:00.257 --> 00:25:02.590 align:middle line:84%
All right, well, you don't
mind, let me forge ahead here

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:05.290 align:middle line:84%
and see how we might
improve this program further

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:07.570 align:middle line:84%
and also introduce a few
other features that we might

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:10.100 align:middle line:90%
want to take into account over time.

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:13.270 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that we can
certainly improve on this program

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:17.020 align:middle line:84%
because it's a little disappointing
that I'm cutting this corner

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:20.680 align:middle line:84%
and saying Hello comma and then
on a new line printing out name.

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:21.700 align:middle line:90%
Like we can do better.

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:24.760 align:middle line:84%
And most programs you use on your
phone or your laptop certainly

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:26.810 align:middle line:90%
keep text together when people want.

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:28.243 align:middle line:90%
So how can we go about doing that?

00:25:28.243 --> 00:25:29.660 align:middle line:90%
Well there's a few different ways.

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:32.890 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, the goal here is not
so much to solve this one problem,

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:35.290 align:middle line:84%
but to demonstrate and
emphasize that in programming--

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:38.080 align:middle line:84%
Python and other languages--
there's so many ways

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:40.690 align:middle line:90%
sometimes to solve the same problem.

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:42.830 align:middle line:84%
And here's one way to
solve this problem.

00:25:42.830 --> 00:25:48.100 align:middle line:84%
Let me go in here and let me
go ahead now and say hello,

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:52.450 align:middle line:84%
and let me just add to the
end of that the user's name.

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:54.980 align:middle line:84%
So I'm using + in kind
of an interesting way.

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:56.560 align:middle line:90%
This is not addition, per se.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:59.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm not adding numbers,
obviously, but I do

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:04.840 align:middle line:84%
kind of want to add the person's name
to the string of text Hello comma.

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:08.830 align:middle line:84%
Well let me go now down to my terminal
window and run Python of Hello.py

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:10.000 align:middle line:90%
again, Enter.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:10.750 align:middle line:90%
What's your name?

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:11.920 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to type in David.

00:26:11.920 --> 00:26:12.940 align:middle line:90%
Enter.

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:14.260 align:middle line:90%
OK, it's better.

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:18.160 align:middle line:84%
It's better, but there's a minor
bug, albeit aesthetic here.

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:20.830 align:middle line:84%
There's missing space, but let's
just use some intuition here.

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:22.900 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I'm missing the
space after the comma,

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:25.780 align:middle line:84%
why don't I go ahead and
just add it manually here.

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:30.670 align:middle line:84%
Let me now rerun the program Python
of Hello.py, Enter, David, Enter.

00:26:30.670 --> 00:26:31.540 align:middle line:90%
And there we go.

00:26:31.540 --> 00:26:33.400 align:middle line:84%
Now we have something that
looks a little prettier

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:34.570 align:middle line:90%
in terms of English grammar.

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:36.640 align:middle line:90%
Hello, comma, space, David.

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:40.060 align:middle line:84%
And now if we rewind, you
might have noticed before

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:45.100 align:middle line:84%
or wondered why I had this seemingly
extra space after my question mark,

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:45.940 align:middle line:90%
namely here.

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:48.790 align:middle line:84%
There's a space after the question
mark but before the double quote

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:50.332 align:middle line:90%
and that was just for aesthetics too.

00:26:50.332 --> 00:26:53.315 align:middle line:84%
I wanted to move the user's
cursor one space to the right

00:26:53.315 --> 00:26:55.690 align:middle line:84%
so that when I typed their
name or they typed their name,

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:59.650 align:middle line:84%
it's not immediately next to
that same question mark there.

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:01.790 align:middle line:90%
But there's other ways we can do this.

00:27:01.790 --> 00:27:05.080 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that some
functions, print among them,

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:07.570 align:middle line:90%
actually take multiple arguments.

00:27:07.570 --> 00:27:11.200 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that if
you separate the inputs

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:14.620 align:middle line:84%
to a function-- the so-called
arguments to a function-- with a comma,

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:19.250 align:middle line:84%
you can pass in not just one, but
two, three, four, five, onward.

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:24.970 align:middle line:84%
So let me go ahead and pass in not just
hello, comma, space, but that followed

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:25.660 align:middle line:90%
by name.

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:27.880 align:middle line:84%
And this is a little confusing
potentially at first glance

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:30.297 align:middle line:84%
because now I've got two commas
but it's important to note

00:27:30.297 --> 00:27:35.800 align:middle line:84%
that the first comma is inside of
my quotation marks, which is simply

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:37.490 align:middle line:90%
an English grammatical thing.

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:40.270 align:middle line:84%
The second comma here is
outside of the quotes,

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:44.170 align:middle line:84%
but between what are now two
separate arguments to print.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:46.270 align:middle line:84%
The first argument is
hello, comma, space.

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:49.490 align:middle line:84%
The second argument is
the name variable itself.

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:51.640 align:middle line:90%
So let's see how this looks.

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.620 align:middle line:90%
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:54.460 align:middle line:90%
What's your name?

00:27:54.460 --> 00:27:55.060 align:middle line:90%
David.

00:27:55.060 --> 00:27:56.050 align:middle line:90%
Enter.

00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:57.910 align:middle line:90%
OK, I've kind of over-corrected.

00:27:57.910 --> 00:28:00.040 align:middle line:90%
Now I've got two spaces for some reason.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:02.080 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out, and
this is subtle, when

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:07.960 align:middle line:84%
you pass multiple arguments to print, it
automatically inserts a space for you.

00:28:07.960 --> 00:28:10.270 align:middle line:84%
This was not relevant
earlier because I was

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:16.510 align:middle line:84%
passing in one big argument to print
all at once by using that + operator.

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:19.070 align:middle line:84%
This time I'm passing in
two because of the comma.

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:23.210 align:middle line:84%
So if I don't want that extra space,
I don't need to pass in one myself,

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:26.530 align:middle line:84%
I can just do this and now notice,
if I run this program again--

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:29.560 align:middle line:84%
Python of Hello.py,
type in my name David,

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:32.620 align:middle line:84%
now it looks grammatically
like I might want.

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:34.330 align:middle line:90%
Now which of these approaches is better?

00:28:34.330 --> 00:28:37.840 align:middle line:84%
This approach uses a function
print with two arguments--

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:40.810 align:middle line:90%
Hello, comma and the name variable.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:44.195 align:middle line:84%
The previous version, recall,
technically used one argument,

00:28:44.195 --> 00:28:45.820 align:middle line:90%
even though it looked a little curious.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:49.270 align:middle line:84%
It's one argument in the sense that
the computer, just like mathematicians,

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:51.950 align:middle line:84%
are going to do what's
inside of parentheses first.

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:54.670 align:middle line:84%
So if inside of parentheses
you have this string of text--

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:58.350 align:middle line:84%
hello, comma, and a space,
which I need to add back.

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:01.050 align:middle line:84%
Then you have a +, which
means not addition, per se,

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:05.400 align:middle line:84%
but concatenation-- to join the thing
on the left and the thing on the right.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:08.400 align:middle line:84%
This ultimately becomes
the English phrase--

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.890 align:middle line:90%
Hello, comma, space, David.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:14.400 align:middle line:84%
And then what's being passed
ultimately to the function

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:18.870 align:middle line:84%
is technically something like this,
but it's doing it all dynamically.

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:23.860 align:middle line:84%
It's not me typing in David
as I discreetly did earlier.

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:27.930 align:middle line:84%
It's figuring out dynamically what
that value is after concatenating Hello

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:30.900 align:middle line:84%
with the value of name and
then passing that ultimately

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:33.750 align:middle line:90%
to print as the sole argument.

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:38.130 align:middle line:84%
Let me pause here to see if there's
any questions on numbers of arguments

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:41.250 align:middle line:90%
now to functions.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:43.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can we use
a function many times

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:48.765 align:middle line:84%
to solve a certain problem which we
can encounter many times in our code?

00:29:48.765 --> 00:29:49.640 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: You can.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:52.700 align:middle line:84%
You can use a function many different
times to solve some problem.

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:55.040 align:middle line:84%
What we'll soon see, though,
is if you find yourself

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:58.620 align:middle line:84%
as the programmer solving a
problem the same way again,

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:00.560 align:middle line:84%
and again, and again,
it turns out you'll

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:02.450 align:middle line:84%
be able to make your
own function so that you

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:07.130 align:middle line:84%
don't have to keep reusing the basic
ones that come with the language.

00:30:07.130 --> 00:30:10.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was curious about
the comma and the + sign.

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:15.320 align:middle line:84%
So after + sign, can we give just
one variable and after comma again we

00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:17.180 align:middle line:90%
give multiple variable status?

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:18.470 align:middle line:90%
What is the difference?

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:19.678 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: A good question.

00:30:19.678 --> 00:30:21.320 align:middle line:90%
So in the context of strings--

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:22.640 align:middle line:90%
and I keep using that term.

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:25.130 align:middle line:84%
"String" is a technical term
in a programming language

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:29.570 align:middle line:84%
and again, it means a sequence of text--
a character, a word, a whole paragraph,

00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:30.080 align:middle line:90%
even.

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:33.650 align:middle line:84%
So the + operator is
not just used, as we'll

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:37.790 align:middle line:84%
see, for addition of numbers in
Python, like we do on paper pencil,

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:41.760 align:middle line:84%
but it also is used for concatenation
of strings on the left and the right.

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:44.180 align:middle line:84%
If you did want to combine
not just two strings--

00:30:44.180 --> 00:30:46.310 align:middle line:84%
left and right-- but
a third and a fourth,

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:51.380 align:middle line:84%
you can absolutely keep using +,
+, +, +, and chain them together

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:52.460 align:middle line:90%
just like in math.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:54.830 align:middle line:84%
Eventually that's going to
start to look a little ugly,

00:30:54.830 --> 00:30:57.080 align:middle line:84%
I dare say, especially if
your line of code gets long.

00:30:57.080 --> 00:31:00.200 align:middle line:84%
So there's better ways that
we'll actually soon see.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:01.670 align:middle line:90%
And a good question as well.

00:31:01.670 --> 00:31:04.190 align:middle line:84%
Well let me come back to
the code here in question

00:31:04.190 --> 00:31:06.800 align:middle line:84%
and see if we can show you
just a couple of other ways

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:10.520 align:middle line:84%
to solve the same problem, along the way
emphasizing that what we're technically

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:12.980 align:middle line:84%
talking about here, yes, are
strings, but there's even

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:15.170 align:middle line:84%
a technical term for
these strings in Python,

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:19.700 align:middle line:84%
it's just STR, so to speak,
S-T-R for short, for string.

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:22.220 align:middle line:84%
As you may know if you
programmed in other languages,

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:26.270 align:middle line:84%
people who invent programming languages
like to be very succinct, to the point,

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:29.780 align:middle line:84%
so we tend to use fairly short
phrases to describe things,

00:31:29.780 --> 00:31:31.370 align:middle line:90%
not necessarily full words.

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:33.920 align:middle line:84%
So while you might say
"string," technically

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:37.070 align:middle line:84%
in Python what we're really talking
about, these sequences of text,

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:38.300 align:middle line:90%
are technically STRs.

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:41.450 align:middle line:84%
This is an actual type
of data in a program.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:45.420 align:middle line:84%
But we'll soon see that there's other
types of data in programs as well.

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:48.980 align:middle line:84%
In fact, let's see if we can't
improve this in one other way.

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:52.550 align:middle line:84%
I like the progress we've made by
keeping everything on the same line--

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:55.490 align:middle line:90%
Hello, David all on the same line.

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:59.070 align:middle line:84%
What more though could we do in
terms of solving this problem?

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:02.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that we
didn't have to give up entirely

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:03.800 align:middle line:90%
with using print twice.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:07.460 align:middle line:84%
Let me rewind a little bit and
go back to that earlier version

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:11.660 align:middle line:84%
where I wasn't really sure how to solve
this problem so I was using print once

00:32:11.660 --> 00:32:14.090 align:middle line:84%
to print out just the Hello
and the space and the comma.

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:17.750 align:middle line:84%
And then I used print
again to print name.

00:32:17.750 --> 00:32:22.220 align:middle line:84%
That's, strictly speaking, wasn't bad,
but there was this visual side effect

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:23.270 align:middle line:90%
that I didn't like.

00:32:23.270 --> 00:32:26.540 align:middle line:84%
It just looked ugly to have
these two lines of text separate

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:27.600 align:middle line:90%
from one another.

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.480 align:middle line:90%
But there's another way to fix this.

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:34.130 align:middle line:84%
Clearly it seems to be the case that
the print function is automatically

00:32:34.130 --> 00:32:36.110 align:middle line:90%
outputting a blank line.

00:32:36.110 --> 00:32:39.380 align:middle line:84%
It's moving the cursor automatically
for me to the next line

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:42.620 align:middle line:84%
because that's why I'm seeing
Hello on one line and David

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:44.150 align:middle line:90%
on the next and then my prompt--

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:46.530 align:middle line:84%
the dollar sign-- on
the line below that.

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:48.800 align:middle line:84%
So print seems to be
presuming automatically

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:51.230 align:middle line:84%
that you want it to move
the cursor to the next line

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:53.040 align:middle line:90%
after you pass it some argument.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:54.890 align:middle line:90%
But you can override that behavior.

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:58.400 align:middle line:84%
Again, functions take arguments
which influence their behavior,

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:00.740 align:middle line:84%
you just have to know
what those arguments are.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:06.560 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that if we look at
the documentation for Python's print

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:09.980 align:middle line:84%
function, we can actually
look up at this URL here--

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:14.150 align:middle line:84%
docs.python.org is where all of
Python's official documentation lies.

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:17.870 align:middle line:84%
If I poke around, I can find my
way to more specifically this URL

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:22.970 align:middle line:84%
here where I can find all of the
available functions in Python

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:24.630 align:middle line:90%
and the documentation there for.

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:26.900 align:middle line:84%
And if I go a little more
precisely, I can even

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:30.230 align:middle line:84%
find specific documentation
for the print function itself.

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:31.980 align:middle line:84%
And rather than pull
that up in a browser,

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:36.050 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go ahead and highlight
just one line from that same URL, which

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:39.890 align:middle line:84%
is this, and this is easily the
most cryptic thing we've seen yet,

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:42.717 align:middle line:84%
but this is the official
documentation for the print function.

00:33:42.717 --> 00:33:45.800 align:middle line:84%
And one of the best things you can do
when learning a programming language

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:48.110 align:middle line:84%
is, honestly, learn to
read the documentation,

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:51.920 align:middle line:84%
because truly, all of the answers
to your questions will in some way

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:55.430 align:middle line:84%
be there, even though, admittedly,
it's not always obvious.

00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:57.290 align:middle line:84%
And I will say too,
Python's documentation

00:33:57.290 --> 00:34:01.460 align:middle line:84%
isn't necessarily the easiest thing,
especially for a first time or novice

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:02.072 align:middle line:90%
programmer.

00:34:02.072 --> 00:34:05.030 align:middle line:84%
It too just takes practice, so try
not to get overwhelmed if you're not

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:06.292 align:middle line:90%
sure what you're looking at.

00:34:06.292 --> 00:34:08.000 align:middle line:84%
But let me walk you
through this example.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:11.360 align:middle line:84%
This again is a line of text from
Python's official documentation

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:12.690 align:middle line:90%
for the print function.

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:14.659 align:middle line:90%
What this indicates as follows is this--

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:17.000 align:middle line:84%
the name of this function
is, of course print.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:20.570 align:middle line:84%
Then there's a parenthesis over here
and another close parenthesis way

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:21.230 align:middle line:90%
over there.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:23.570 align:middle line:84%
Everything inside of
those parentheses are

00:34:23.570 --> 00:34:27.270 align:middle line:84%
the arguments, the potential
arguments, to the function.

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:30.830 align:middle line:84%
However, when we're
looking at these arguments

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:34.400 align:middle line:84%
in the documentation like this,
there's technically a different term

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:35.150 align:middle line:90%
that we would use.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:37.920 align:middle line:84%
These are technically the
parameters to the function.

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:41.900 align:middle line:84%
So when you're talking about
what you can pass to a function

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:45.679 align:middle line:84%
and what those inputs are
called, those are parameters.

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:48.080 align:middle line:84%
When you actually use
the function and pass

00:34:48.080 --> 00:34:51.650 align:middle line:84%
in values inside of those
parentheses, those inputs,

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:53.580 align:middle line:90%
those values are arguments.

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:56.719 align:middle line:84%
So we're talking about the exact same
thing-- parameters and arguments are

00:34:56.719 --> 00:34:58.850 align:middle line:84%
effectively the same
thing, but the terms you

00:34:58.850 --> 00:35:01.520 align:middle line:84%
use from looking at the problem
from different directions.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:04.220 align:middle line:84%
When we're looking at what the
function can take versus what

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:06.210 align:middle line:84%
you're actually passing
into the function.

00:35:06.210 --> 00:35:07.500 align:middle line:90%
So what does this imply?

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:10.610 align:middle line:84%
Well this syntax is pretty
cryptic, but at the moment,

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:14.360 align:middle line:84%
just know that an asterisk, a
star, and then the word "objects"

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:17.330 align:middle line:84%
means that the print function
can take any number of objects.

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:20.540 align:middle line:84%
You can pass in 0 strings
of text, one string

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:23.900 align:middle line:84%
like I did, two strings like I did,
or, technically, infinitely many

00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:27.170 align:middle line:84%
if you really want, though that
code is not going to look very good.

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:31.130 align:middle line:84%
After that we see a comma, then
we see another parameter here

00:35:31.130 --> 00:35:34.400 align:middle line:84%
called SEP, short for
separator in English.

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:38.640 align:middle line:84%
And notice the equal sign and the
single quote, space, single quote.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:42.350 align:middle line:84%
So 'space' I don't
know what that is yet,

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:44.570 align:middle line:90%
but I think we've seen a hint about it.

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:46.430 align:middle line:84%
Let's focus though
for a moment on this--

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:50.720 align:middle line:84%
the print function takes another
parameter called end and the default

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:54.560 align:middle line:84%
value of that parameter is apparently,
based on this equal sign and these

00:35:54.560 --> 00:35:57.020 align:middle line:90%
quotes, /n.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:00.260 align:middle line:84%
And what is /n, if you'd
like to chime in in the chat?

00:36:00.260 --> 00:36:02.810 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who's program before
has probably seen this,

00:36:02.810 --> 00:36:07.070 align:middle line:84%
though if you've never programmed
before, this might look quite cryptic.

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:12.530 align:middle line:84%
Backslash n means new line,
and it's a way textually

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:15.710 align:middle line:84%
of indicating if and when you
want the computer effectively

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:19.490 align:middle line:84%
to move the cursor to the next
line, create a new line of text.

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:22.310 align:middle line:84%
And so technically, if we
read into the documentation,

00:36:22.310 --> 00:36:23.960 align:middle line:90%
we'll see more detail on this.

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:28.550 align:middle line:84%
The fact that there's a parameter called
end in the documentation for the print

00:36:28.550 --> 00:36:32.840 align:middle line:84%
function, just means that by default
this print function is going to end

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:35.240 align:middle line:90%
every line with /n.

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:37.670 align:middle line:84%
You don't literally see
/n, you see a new line.

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:40.320 align:middle line:84%
You see the cursor
moving to the next line.

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:42.500 align:middle line:90%
Now by that logic, let's move backwards.

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:46.790 align:middle line:84%
SEP four separator-- the
default value of separator

00:36:46.790 --> 00:36:49.460 align:middle line:90%
is apparently a single blank space.

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:51.020 align:middle line:90%
Well where have we seen that?

00:36:51.020 --> 00:36:55.190 align:middle line:84%
Well recall in an earlier example
when I passed in not just one but two

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:57.210 align:middle line:90%
arguments to the print function.

00:36:57.210 --> 00:37:00.300 align:middle line:84%
Recall that they magically
had a space between them.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:02.540 align:middle line:84%
In fact, they had that
space + my own space

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:05.720 align:middle line:84%
and that's why I deleted my space
because at that point it was extra.

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:09.620 align:middle line:84%
So this just means that when you pass
multiple arguments to print, by default

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:11.750 align:middle line:84%
they're going to be
separated by a single space.

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:14.570 align:middle line:84%
By default, when you
pass arguments to print,

00:37:14.570 --> 00:37:17.880 align:middle line:84%
it's the whole thing is going
to be ended with a new line.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.850 align:middle line:84%
Now just by knowing this,
and let me literally

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:22.890 align:middle line:84%
wave my hand at the rest of the
documentation for another day,

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:24.890 align:middle line:84%
there's more things that
print can do, but we're

00:37:24.890 --> 00:37:29.360 align:middle line:84%
going to focus just on SEP and on END,
let's see if we can't leverage this now

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:31.010 align:middle line:90%
to solve that original problem.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:34.250 align:middle line:84%
The original problem was this,
I don't like how Hello, David

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:35.750 align:middle line:90%
is on two different lines.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:38.420 align:middle line:84%
Well that's happening again
because print is automatically

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:41.810 align:middle line:84%
printing out a new line, so
let's tell it not to do that.

00:37:41.810 --> 00:37:47.150 align:middle line:84%
Let's tell it by passing a second
argument to the first use of PRINT

00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:51.110 align:middle line:90%
to say END = quote, unquote--

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:54.840 align:middle line:84%
not /n, which is the
default automatically.

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:58.250 align:middle line:84%
Let's make it quote,
unquote nothing else.

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:01.850 align:middle line:84%
Let's override the default
value so there is no new line.

00:38:01.850 --> 00:38:03.560 align:middle line:90%
There's literally nothing there.

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:05.250 align:middle line:90%
And let's see what happens.

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:08.270 align:middle line:84%
Let me now go down to my
terminal window and clear it.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:10.672 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to run
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:38:10.672 --> 00:38:12.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to type in
my name, David and I

00:38:12.380 --> 00:38:16.280 align:middle line:84%
think now everything is going to
stay on the same line because--

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:22.460 align:middle line:84%
and it did-- this line here, 5, is
going to print out Hello, comma, space,

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:24.710 align:middle line:84%
but then nothing at
the end of it because I

00:38:24.710 --> 00:38:26.450 align:middle line:90%
changed it to be quote, unquote.

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:29.210 align:middle line:84%
The second line is going
to print the name, David,

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:32.780 align:middle line:84%
or whatever the human's name is, and it
will move the cursor to the next line

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:36.230 align:middle line:84%
because I didn't override
the value of END there.

00:38:36.230 --> 00:38:39.710 align:middle line:84%
Just to see this more explicitly,
if you do something cryptic like,

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:41.420 align:middle line:90%
well I have no idea what's going on.

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:44.120 align:middle line:84%
Let me just put in temporarily
three question marks here.

00:38:44.120 --> 00:38:46.040 align:middle line:90%
We'll see the results of this too.

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:49.202 align:middle line:84%
Let me go back down to my terminal
window, run Python of hello. py,

00:38:49.202 --> 00:38:49.910 align:middle line:90%
what's your name?

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:50.420 align:middle line:90%
David.

00:38:50.420 --> 00:38:54.207 align:middle line:84%
And now you see literally
really ugly output,

00:38:54.207 --> 00:38:55.790 align:middle line:90%
but you see literally what's going on.

00:38:55.790 --> 00:39:01.010 align:middle line:84%
Hello, comma, space, then three question
marks, END, that print statement,

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:05.570 align:middle line:84%
and then you see D-A-V-I-D.
So not a good outcome,

00:39:05.570 --> 00:39:09.080 align:middle line:84%
but it demonstrates just how
much control we have here too.

00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:10.490 align:middle line:90%
And let me rewind further.

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:13.250 align:middle line:84%
Recall that in our
other version of this,

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:18.710 align:middle line:84%
when I passed in Hello, comma and name,
they were separated by a single space.

00:39:18.710 --> 00:39:22.340 align:middle line:90%
So Python of Hello.py, D-A-V-I-D, Enter.

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:23.690 align:middle line:90%
That just worked.

00:39:23.690 --> 00:39:27.950 align:middle line:84%
Well what if we override the
value of SEP for separator?

00:39:27.950 --> 00:39:31.730 align:middle line:84%
Instead of being one space,
we could say something

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:35.030 align:middle line:84%
like, question mark, question
mark, question mark just to wrap

00:39:35.030 --> 00:39:37.110 align:middle line:90%
our minds around what's going on there.

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:41.300 align:middle line:84%
Let me now do Python of
Hello.py, D-A-V-I-D, Enter.

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:42.650 align:middle line:90%
And you see two.

00:39:42.650 --> 00:39:43.850 align:middle line:90%
These two inputs--

00:39:43.850 --> 00:39:47.840 align:middle line:84%
Hello, comma and the name are
now separated in an ugly way

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:52.580 align:middle line:84%
by three question marks because I've
overridden the default behavior of SEP.

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:54.803 align:middle line:84%
And even though the
documentation uses single quotes,

00:39:54.803 --> 00:39:56.720 align:middle line:84%
I've been in the habit
of using double quotes.

00:39:56.720 --> 00:39:58.580 align:middle line:90%
In Python you can use either.

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:01.620 align:middle line:84%
Strictly speaking, it doesn't
matter, but you should be consistent

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:03.530 align:middle line:84%
and I generally always
use double quotes.

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:08.240 align:middle line:84%
Python's documentation, though,
always uses single quotes.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:12.320 align:middle line:84%
Questions now on these
types of parameters?

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:15.290 align:middle line:84%
And allow me to propose that
we give these an official name.

00:40:15.290 --> 00:40:19.220 align:middle line:84%
Up until now, when we've
been passing values to print,

00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:22.160 align:middle line:84%
those are called positional
parameters-- positional in the sense

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:24.620 align:middle line:84%
that the first thing you pass
to print gets printed first.

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:27.830 align:middle line:84%
The second thing you pass to print
after a comma gets printed second.

00:40:27.830 --> 00:40:28.710 align:middle line:90%
And so forth.

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:32.210 align:middle line:84%
But there's also these things we've
now seen called named parameters.

00:40:32.210 --> 00:40:37.550 align:middle line:84%
Named SEP, separator, or END,
E-N-D for the line ending.

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:40.910 align:middle line:84%
Those are named parameters
because one, they're optional

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:44.450 align:middle line:84%
and you can pass them in at the
end of your print statement,

00:40:44.450 --> 00:40:48.290 align:middle line:90%
but you can also use them by name.

00:40:48.290 --> 00:40:51.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This may be a weird
question, but I was wondering,

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:57.350 align:middle line:84%
what if someone wants to add actually
quotation marks within the quotation

00:40:57.350 --> 00:40:57.920 align:middle line:90%
marks?

00:40:57.920 --> 00:40:59.600 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, I like how you think.

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:01.880 align:middle line:84%
This is what we would
call a corner case, right?

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:03.860 align:middle line:84%
Just when we've made--
right, this is all

00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:06.823 align:middle line:84%
sounding great, at least as
programming goes, but, wait a minute,

00:41:06.823 --> 00:41:08.240 align:middle line:90%
what if you want to print a quote?

00:41:08.240 --> 00:41:09.557 align:middle line:90%
That's a really good question.

00:41:09.557 --> 00:41:11.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's see if we
can't figure this out.

00:41:11.390 --> 00:41:15.590 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that I want to print
out not just the user's name.

00:41:15.590 --> 00:41:16.940 align:middle line:90%
Let me simplify this further.

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:18.980 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and get
rid of a lot of this,

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:21.510 align:middle line:84%
and let me just say
something like, Hello--

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:24.170 align:middle line:90%


00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:26.240 align:middle line:90%
maybe I'm being a little sarcastic here.

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:28.310 align:middle line:90%
Hello, (sarcastically) "friend."

00:41:28.310 --> 00:41:30.022 align:middle line:90%
You know, in that kind of tone.

00:41:30.022 --> 00:41:31.730 align:middle line:84%
Well this is not going
to work, actually,

00:41:31.730 --> 00:41:36.260 align:middle line:84%
because you are trying to use quotes
to be like "friend" in finger quotes,

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:38.070 align:middle line:84%
but you're also trying
to end the sentence.

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:39.778 align:middle line:84%
And if I try running
this, let's do this.

00:41:39.778 --> 00:41:43.160 align:middle line:84%
Python of Hello.py, you'll see
that this is just invalid syntax.

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:44.420 align:middle line:90%
Perhaps you forgot a comma.

00:41:44.420 --> 00:41:45.920 align:middle line:90%
And this is actually a bit annoying.

00:41:45.920 --> 00:41:48.822 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes the error messages
you see are misleading.

00:41:48.822 --> 00:41:50.780 align:middle line:84%
Like the computer, the
language, doesn't really

00:41:50.780 --> 00:41:52.488 align:middle line:84%
know what's going on
so it gives its best

00:41:52.488 --> 00:41:55.010 align:middle line:90%
guess, but it's not necessarily correct.

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:57.870 align:middle line:84%
But I can solve this
problem in a couple of ways.

00:41:57.870 --> 00:41:59.160 align:middle line:90%
I can do this--

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:01.912 align:middle line:84%
I can change my outermost
quotes to single quotes.

00:42:01.912 --> 00:42:03.620 align:middle line:84%
Because recall a moment
again, I said you

00:42:03.620 --> 00:42:07.130 align:middle line:84%
could use double quotes or single
quotes so long as you're consistent.

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:08.000 align:middle line:90%
So that's fine.

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:10.142 align:middle line:84%
If you use single quotes
on the outside, you

00:42:10.142 --> 00:42:13.100 align:middle line:84%
can then use double quotes on the
inside and you'll see them literally.

00:42:13.100 --> 00:42:17.000 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, if I run
Python of Hello.py, there we go.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:18.590 align:middle line:90%
Hello, "friend."

00:42:18.590 --> 00:42:19.910 align:middle line:90%
But there's another way.

00:42:19.910 --> 00:42:23.450 align:middle line:84%
If you insist on using double
quotes as you might want to,

00:42:23.450 --> 00:42:27.800 align:middle line:84%
just to be consistent, you can also
use that backslash character again.

00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:30.080 align:middle line:90%
We saw the /n a moment ago.

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:33.380 align:middle line:84%
And that meant we don't want a
literal n to be in the output,

00:42:33.380 --> 00:42:34.640 align:middle line:90%
we wanted a new line.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:38.420 align:middle line:84%
So the backslash actually represents
what's called an escape character.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.430 align:middle line:84%
An escape character is
one that you can't just

00:42:40.430 --> 00:42:43.130 align:middle line:90%
type necessarily once on your keyboard.

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:45.270 align:middle line:84%
You need to express it
with multiple characters.

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:50.920 align:middle line:84%
So I can actually put backslashes in
front of these inner double quotes

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:52.920 align:middle line:84%
so that the computer
realizes, Oh wait a minute,

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:57.560 align:middle line:84%
those aren't quotes that finish or start
the thought, they're literal quotes.

00:42:57.560 --> 00:43:02.420 align:middle line:84%
So now let me go back to my terminal
window, run Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:04.800 align:middle line:90%
And now it's working as well.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:08.390 align:middle line:84%
So escaping is a general technique
that allows us to do that too.

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:12.230 align:middle line:84%
And if I may, let me rewind
now on these examples

00:43:12.230 --> 00:43:14.810 align:middle line:84%
and go back to where we
left off with my code,

00:43:14.810 --> 00:43:18.200 align:middle line:84%
I'm just undoing all of that, because
I want to get back to the point

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:23.820 align:middle line:84%
ultimately of specifying now a
final way of solving this problem.

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:26.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that
we have yet another way

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:30.680 align:middle line:84%
we can solve this problem, which
is perhaps the most frequently done

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:35.030 align:middle line:84%
now or at least the most elegant
when it comes to setting us up

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:38.840 align:middle line:90%
for longer and longer uses of strings.

00:43:38.840 --> 00:43:43.620 align:middle line:84%
You can use a relatively new feature
of Python that allows you to do this.

00:43:43.620 --> 00:43:47.397 align:middle line:84%
You can literally put, not the name of
the variable like that in your string,

00:43:47.397 --> 00:43:49.230 align:middle line:84%
because we already saw
this is wrong, right?

00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:52.340 align:middle line:84%
If you do this, you will
literally see Hello, comma, name.

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:53.580 align:middle line:90%
But what if I do this?

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:58.070 align:middle line:84%
What if I put curly braces or curly
brackets around the variable's name?

00:43:58.070 --> 00:44:00.830 align:middle line:84%
Notice VS Code is actually very
subtly changing the color of it.

00:44:00.830 --> 00:44:03.590 align:middle line:84%
So VS Code knows something
interesting is going on here.

00:44:03.590 --> 00:44:08.060 align:middle line:84%
Let me run this program, but I'm not
done yet Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:44:08.060 --> 00:44:09.650 align:middle line:90%
D-A-V-I-D, Enter.

00:44:09.650 --> 00:44:13.430 align:middle line:84%
OK, obviously not what I want,
but I need to tell Python

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:15.140 align:middle line:90%
that this is a special string.

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:18.470 align:middle line:84%
This is what we're going to call
a format string or an F string,

00:44:18.470 --> 00:44:21.660 align:middle line:84%
a relatively new feature of
Python in the past few years

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:26.750 align:middle line:84%
that tells Python to actually format
stuff in the string in a special way.

00:44:26.750 --> 00:44:29.480 align:middle line:84%
And the symbol via what you
do this is a little weird,

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:31.220 align:middle line:90%
but this is what the world chose.

00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:35.390 align:middle line:84%
If you put an F at the
beginning of the string,

00:44:35.390 --> 00:44:40.160 align:middle line:84%
right before the first quote mark,
that's a clue to Python that, ooh,

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:41.210 align:middle line:90%
this is a special string.

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:43.550 align:middle line:84%
Let me format this in
a special way for you.

00:44:43.550 --> 00:44:45.380 align:middle line:90%
Let me now rerun the program.

00:44:45.380 --> 00:44:47.120 align:middle line:90%
Python Hello.py, Enter.

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:48.410 align:middle line:90%
D-A-V-I-D, Enter.

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:51.170 align:middle line:84%
And now we see the
goal this whole time--

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:52.395 align:middle line:90%
Hello, David.

00:44:52.395 --> 00:44:55.520 align:middle line:84%
We don't start with this way, because
I think if we did this the first way,

00:44:55.520 --> 00:44:57.330 align:middle line:90%
you'd be like, why are we doing this?

00:44:57.330 --> 00:44:58.850 align:middle line:90%
What are all these magical symbols?

00:44:58.850 --> 00:45:02.370 align:middle line:84%
But this is just yet another
way to solve the same problem.

00:45:02.370 --> 00:45:04.940 align:middle line:84%
But let me propose that
we consider now yet

00:45:04.940 --> 00:45:06.920 align:middle line:90%
other things we can do with strings.

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:11.190 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that even as we've been
doing some relatively simple operations

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:14.665 align:middle line:84%
here, we've generally been trusting
that the user is going to cooperate

00:45:14.665 --> 00:45:17.790 align:middle line:84%
and that is to say that they're going
to actually type in what we want them

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:18.450 align:middle line:90%
to type.

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:20.850 align:middle line:84%
Now just because they type a
string, though, doesn't mean

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:22.410 align:middle line:90%
it's going to look the way we want.

00:45:22.410 --> 00:45:24.510 align:middle line:84%
You and I, honestly,
as humans are actually

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:27.840 align:middle line:84%
in the habit-- on websites and
apps-- of like accidentally hitting

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:30.300 align:middle line:84%
the spacebar a lot, either
at the beginning of our input

00:45:30.300 --> 00:45:33.460 align:middle line:84%
or at the end, maybe because the
space bar tends to be so big.

00:45:33.460 --> 00:45:35.790 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty common to
get accidental spaces

00:45:35.790 --> 00:45:37.950 align:middle line:90%
before or after some users' input.

00:45:37.950 --> 00:45:41.675 align:middle line:84%
You and I are definitely in the habit
of not necessarily capitalizing words

00:45:41.675 --> 00:45:42.300 align:middle line:90%
like we should.

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:45.122 align:middle line:84%
If we're sending text messages,
we're probably being a little quick

00:45:45.122 --> 00:45:46.830 align:middle line:84%
and just sending
everything in lowercase,

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:49.913 align:middle line:84%
for instance, if that's your style,
if your phone's not fixing it for you.

00:45:49.913 --> 00:45:52.530 align:middle line:84%
Maybe in a formal letter you
would capitalize things properly.

00:45:52.530 --> 00:45:56.400 align:middle line:84%
But you and I as humans, we can't really
be trusted to type things in a nice way

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:58.870 align:middle line:84%
necessarily when using
some piece of software,

00:45:58.870 --> 00:46:00.940 align:middle line:84%
be it an app or a website
or something else.

00:46:00.940 --> 00:46:04.560 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that
strings themselves come

00:46:04.560 --> 00:46:06.450 align:middle line:90%
with a lot of built-in functionality.

00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:09.300 align:middle line:84%
You can see all of that in
Python's own documentation here.

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:12.600 align:middle line:84%
The string data type that
we've been talking about

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:14.970 align:middle line:84%
comes with a lot of
functionality built in that

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:18.360 align:middle line:84%
means that we can manipulate the
user's input to do more than just

00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:22.290 align:middle line:84%
join it with something else like
Hello, we can actually clean it up

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:26.380 align:middle line:84%
or reformat it in a way that hopefully
looks a little better for us.

00:46:26.380 --> 00:46:29.070 align:middle line:84%
So let me go back to my
code here and let me just

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:32.100 align:middle line:84%
demonstrate what might happen
if a user doesn't cooperate.

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:35.490 align:middle line:84%
If I go ahead here and run
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:38.890 align:middle line:84%
Let me just sloppily hit the
spacebar a few too many times.

00:46:38.890 --> 00:46:39.390 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:40.890 align:middle line:90%
I just wasn't paying attention.

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:43.710 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to type in my
name D-A-V-I-D and, I don't know,

00:46:43.710 --> 00:46:45.510 align:middle line:90%
I hit the spacebar a couple more times.

00:46:45.510 --> 00:46:46.620 align:middle line:90%
Like it's kind of a mess.

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:47.785 align:middle line:90%
It's all lowercase.

00:46:47.785 --> 00:46:50.160 align:middle line:84%
That's not going to necessarily
look grammatically right.

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:51.900 align:middle line:90%
It's got spaces here and here.

00:46:51.900 --> 00:46:54.030 align:middle line:84%
The program is going
to print exactly that

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:55.890 align:middle line:84%
and that looks really
bad, at least if we're

00:46:55.890 --> 00:46:57.900 align:middle line:90%
prioritizing aesthetics and grammar.

00:46:57.900 --> 00:47:00.330 align:middle line:84%
Like, why are there so many
spaces after the comma?

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:03.420 align:middle line:84%
This is not a very nice
way to greet your users.

00:47:03.420 --> 00:47:05.130 align:middle line:90%
But we can clean this up.

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:10.170 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that built into strings,
which, again, is this data type, so

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:12.780 align:middle line:90%
to speak, this type of data in Python--

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:15.730 align:middle line:84%
is the ability to actually
do things to that string.

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:20.280 align:middle line:84%
So let me do this, I can actually go
ahead and do something like this--

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:23.820 align:middle line:90%
name = name.strip.

00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:25.240 align:middle line:90%
And what does this do?

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:30.460 align:middle line:90%
Remove white space from string.

00:47:30.460 --> 00:47:32.060 align:middle line:90%
And what do I mean by this?

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:33.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, on the right-hand
side, notice I've

00:47:33.910 --> 00:47:36.700 align:middle line:90%
written the variable name called Name.

00:47:36.700 --> 00:47:40.120 align:middle line:84%
I've then used a period
or a dot, and then I

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:42.520 align:middle line:84%
seem to be doing what's
a function, right?

00:47:42.520 --> 00:47:46.300 align:middle line:84%
Any time we've seen a function thus
far, we see the function's name-- print

00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:49.030 align:middle line:84%
or input, then we see a parenthesis,
then another parenthesis,

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:50.678 align:middle line:90%
and that's exactly what I see here.

00:47:50.678 --> 00:47:52.720 align:middle line:84%
But I'm using this function
a little differently.

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:55.990 align:middle line:84%
Technically, this function is--
in this context-- called a method.

00:47:55.990 --> 00:47:57.620 align:middle line:90%
And what do I mean by that?

00:47:57.620 --> 00:48:00.250 align:middle line:90%
Well, if Name is a string, a.k.a.

00:48:00.250 --> 00:48:03.130 align:middle line:84%
STR, well it turns out,
according to the documentation,

00:48:03.130 --> 00:48:07.630 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot of functions that
come with strings in Python.

00:48:07.630 --> 00:48:10.150 align:middle line:84%
And you can access that
functionality by using

00:48:10.150 --> 00:48:14.860 align:middle line:84%
the name of a string like literally
name here, then a period, then the name

00:48:14.860 --> 00:48:18.220 align:middle line:84%
of the, function and then an open
parenthesis and a closed parenthesis.

00:48:18.220 --> 00:48:21.320 align:middle line:84%
Maybe some arguments inside of
those parentheses, but in this case,

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:22.900 align:middle line:90%
it doesn't need any arguments.

00:48:22.900 --> 00:48:26.320 align:middle line:84%
I just want to strip the space
from the left and the space

00:48:26.320 --> 00:48:28.000 align:middle line:90%
from the right of the user's input.

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:29.210 align:middle line:90%
But that's not enough.

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:31.120 align:middle line:84%
I want to remember
that I've stripped off

00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:32.920 align:middle line:84%
that white space on
the left and the right,

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:35.470 align:middle line:84%
so I'm going to use the
equal sign again here.

00:48:35.470 --> 00:48:38.590 align:middle line:84%
And notice that just as before,
this doesn't mean equality,

00:48:38.590 --> 00:48:40.940 align:middle line:84%
this means assignment
from right to left.

00:48:40.940 --> 00:48:43.900 align:middle line:84%
So when this line of
code here-- name.strip--

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:46.600 align:middle line:90%
returns to me, a.k.a.

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:49.123 align:middle line:84%
a return value, it will
return the same thing

00:48:49.123 --> 00:48:51.790 align:middle line:84%
that the user typed in, but with
no more white space to the left

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:54.370 align:middle line:90%
or to the white [CHUCKLES] to the right.

00:48:54.370 --> 00:48:56.860 align:middle line:84%
So then the equal sign
assignment is going

00:48:56.860 --> 00:49:00.190 align:middle line:84%
to copy that value from
the right to the left,

00:49:00.190 --> 00:49:04.820 align:middle line:84%
thereby updating the value
inside of my name variable.

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:07.240 align:middle line:84%
So you can not only assign
values to variables,

00:49:07.240 --> 00:49:10.300 align:middle line:84%
you can absolutely change
the value of variables

00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:12.817 align:middle line:84%
by just using the assignment
operator, the equal sign again,

00:49:12.817 --> 00:49:15.400 align:middle line:84%
and again, and again, and it
will just keep copying from right

00:49:15.400 --> 00:49:17.810 align:middle line:84%
to left whatever the
new value should be.

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:23.740 align:middle line:84%
So now if I rerun this program,
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:49:23.740 --> 00:49:25.930 align:middle line:90%
I have D-A-V-I-- oop, let's do it again.

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:29.170 align:middle line:84%
Space, space, space, space, space.
d-a-v-i-d in all lowercase, space,

00:49:29.170 --> 00:49:30.760 align:middle line:90%
space, Enter.

00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:32.060 align:middle line:90%
It's better.

00:49:32.060 --> 00:49:34.300 align:middle line:84%
It hasn't fixed my
capitalization, so I'm still

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:38.050 align:middle line:84%
being a little sloppy with the
first D, But it has stripped off

00:49:38.050 --> 00:49:39.430 align:middle line:90%
all of that extra space.

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:41.170 align:middle line:90%
Super minor detail, right?

00:49:41.170 --> 00:49:44.050 align:middle line:84%
Like this isn't all that
exciting, but it just

00:49:44.050 --> 00:49:47.440 align:middle line:84%
speaks to the power of what you can
do with just a single line of code.

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:49.160 align:middle line:90%
Now what else can I do here?

00:49:49.160 --> 00:49:51.610 align:middle line:84%
Well I could capitalize
the user's input.

00:49:51.610 --> 00:49:53.390 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead and try this.

00:49:53.390 --> 00:49:59.810 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that I could also
do this-- name.capitalize.

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:03.420 align:middle line:84%
So let me go ahead and
capitalize user's name.

00:50:03.420 --> 00:50:05.170 align:middle line:84%
And again, I'm making
comments and there's

00:50:05.170 --> 00:50:06.820 align:middle line:90%
no one right way to write the comments.

00:50:06.820 --> 00:50:08.770 align:middle line:84%
I'm just using some short
English phrases here

00:50:08.770 --> 00:50:10.510 align:middle line:90%
to remind myself of what I'm doing.

00:50:10.510 --> 00:50:12.190 align:middle line:90%
What's now going on here?

00:50:12.190 --> 00:50:15.310 align:middle line:84%
Well let me go ahead and run
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

00:50:15.310 --> 00:50:19.240 align:middle line:84%
Space, space, space, space, space,
d-a-v-i-d, space, space, Enter.

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:19.960 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:19.960 --> 00:50:21.580 align:middle line:90%
Now it's looking prettier, right?

00:50:21.580 --> 00:50:24.640 align:middle line:84%
No matter how the user typed in
their name, even a little sloppily,

00:50:24.640 --> 00:50:25.720 align:middle line:90%
I'm now fixing that.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:27.313 align:middle line:90%
But let's try something.

00:50:27.313 --> 00:50:28.730 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting a little curious here.

00:50:28.730 --> 00:50:29.830 align:middle line:90%
How about this?

00:50:29.830 --> 00:50:33.910 align:middle line:84%
Space, space, space, space,
space, d-a-v-i-d space m-a-l-a-n--

00:50:33.910 --> 00:50:35.530 align:middle line:90%
I'll use my last name now--

00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:36.640 align:middle line:90%
Enter.

00:50:36.640 --> 00:50:42.100 align:middle line:84%
OK, so ironically, "capitalize" is not
really capitalizing everything we want.

00:50:42.100 --> 00:50:43.870 align:middle line:90%
It's clearly capitalizing what?

00:50:43.870 --> 00:50:45.580 align:middle line:90%
Just the very first letter.

00:50:45.580 --> 00:50:48.940 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that, again,
there's other functions in Python

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:49.960 align:middle line:90%
that come with strings.

00:50:49.960 --> 00:50:54.670 align:middle line:84%
And if we poke around the documentation,
scrolling through a URL like that,

00:50:54.670 --> 00:50:58.540 align:middle line:84%
I bet we'll find another solution,
one of which is actually this.

00:50:58.540 --> 00:51:01.180 align:middle line:90%
Let's actually change this to title.

00:51:01.180 --> 00:51:03.550 align:middle line:84%
There's yet another function
that comes with strings

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:07.120 align:middle line:84%
called Title that do
title-based capitalization, just

00:51:07.120 --> 00:51:09.310 align:middle line:84%
like a book or a person's
name, capitalizing

00:51:09.310 --> 00:51:12.010 align:middle line:90%
the first letter of each word.

00:51:12.010 --> 00:51:14.450 align:middle line:84%
And this is just going to do
a little more work for us.

00:51:14.450 --> 00:51:15.880 align:middle line:90%
So let's go ahead and run this.

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:18.700 align:middle line:84%
And as an aside, I'm kind
of tired now at this point

00:51:18.700 --> 00:51:22.130 align:middle line:84%
of typing Python, Python,
Python all the time.

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:25.570 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that when using a
command line interface like this,

00:51:25.570 --> 00:51:28.000 align:middle line:84%
you can actually go back through
all of your old commands.

00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:30.280 align:middle line:84%
What I just did a moment
ago was I hit the up arrow.

00:51:30.280 --> 00:51:33.820 align:middle line:84%
That immediately goes back through
my history of all of the commands

00:51:33.820 --> 00:51:34.720 align:middle line:90%
I've ever typed.

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:37.960 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a faster way
now for me to repeat myself

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:39.700 align:middle line:90%
than typing everything manually.

00:51:39.700 --> 00:51:42.850 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and hit Enter,
space, space, space, space, space,

00:51:42.850 --> 00:51:47.110 align:middle line:84%
d-a-v-i-d m-a-l-a-n space,
space, all lowercase, Enter.

00:51:47.110 --> 00:51:49.690 align:middle line:90%
Now it's looking better.

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:52.030 align:middle line:84%
Now I've capitalized things
and cleaned things up.

00:51:52.030 --> 00:51:53.200 align:middle line:90%
But what about my code?

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:55.930 align:middle line:84%
I've got like eight lines
of code now, four of which

00:51:55.930 --> 00:51:58.030 align:middle line:84%
are comments, four of
which are actual code.

00:51:58.030 --> 00:51:59.710 align:middle line:90%
Do I really need this much?

00:51:59.710 --> 00:52:00.910 align:middle line:90%
Well, not necessarily.

00:52:00.910 --> 00:52:03.520 align:middle line:90%
Watch what I can also do in Python.

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:07.100 align:middle line:84%
Let me not bother capitalizing
the user's name separately.

00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:08.170 align:middle line:90%
Let me say this--

00:52:08.170 --> 00:52:12.970 align:middle line:90%
and capitalize user's name.

00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:15.370 align:middle line:90%
I can chain these functions together.

00:52:15.370 --> 00:52:18.130 align:middle line:90%
I can add Title to the end of this.

00:52:18.130 --> 00:52:19.600 align:middle line:90%
And now what's happening?

00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:21.440 align:middle line:84%
Well again, with a
line of code like this,

00:52:21.440 --> 00:52:24.490 align:middle line:84%
you first focus on what's to
the right of the equal sign,

00:52:24.490 --> 00:52:26.470 align:middle line:84%
then we'll get to the
left of the equal sign.

00:52:26.470 --> 00:52:28.095 align:middle line:90%
What's on the right of the equals sign?

00:52:28.095 --> 00:52:29.240 align:middle line:90%
This line here.

00:52:29.240 --> 00:52:30.390 align:middle line:90%
Well what does this mean?

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:35.610 align:middle line:84%
Get the value of the name variable,
like D-A-V-I-D space, M-A-L-A-N.

00:52:35.610 --> 00:52:38.700 align:middle line:84%
Then strip off the white space
on the left and the right.

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:40.920 align:middle line:90%
That is going to return a value.

00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:44.790 align:middle line:84%
It's going to return D-A-V-I-D space
M-A-L-A-N without any white space

00:52:44.790 --> 00:52:45.720 align:middle line:90%
to the left or right.

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:47.940 align:middle line:84%
What do you want to do
with that return value?

00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:50.850 align:middle line:84%
You want Python to
title case it, that is

00:52:50.850 --> 00:52:54.330 align:middle line:84%
go through every word
in that resulting string

00:52:54.330 --> 00:52:57.000 align:middle line:84%
and fix the first letter of the
first word, the first letter

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:58.390 align:middle line:90%
of the second word, and so forth.

00:52:58.390 --> 00:53:00.330 align:middle line:84%
And then-- now we can
finish our thought--

00:53:00.330 --> 00:53:05.580 align:middle line:84%
copy the whole thing from right to
left into that same name variable.

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:06.330 align:middle line:90%
And you know what?

00:53:06.330 --> 00:53:08.340 align:middle line:90%
I can take this even one step further.

00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:11.400 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we go ahead
and do this if we want.

00:53:11.400 --> 00:53:16.890 align:middle line:84%
Let me get rid of all of that,
and let me just do strip and title

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:18.270 align:middle line:90%
all on that first line.

00:53:18.270 --> 00:53:21.380 align:middle line:84%
And now we've gone from like
eight lines of code to four.

00:53:21.380 --> 00:53:22.740 align:middle line:90%
It's a lot tighter.

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:23.880 align:middle line:90%
It's a lot neater.

00:53:23.880 --> 00:53:26.250 align:middle line:84%
And even though reasonable
people might disagree,

00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:30.090 align:middle line:84%
it's arguably better because
it's just easier to read.

00:53:30.090 --> 00:53:32.670 align:middle line:84%
Fewer lines of code, fewer
opportunities for mistakes,

00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:37.350 align:middle line:84%
it just allows me to move on
with my next problem to solve.

00:53:37.350 --> 00:53:41.730 align:middle line:84%
All right, let me pause here and see if
there's any questions on these methods.

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:45.540 align:middle line:84%
A method is a function that's
built in to a type of value,

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:47.820 align:middle line:90%
like these functions are.

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:50.640 align:middle line:84%
Or on F strings which
we saw a moment ago.

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:51.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes, hi.

