00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:22.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How many functions can be
combine like this dot strip, dot title,

00:54:22.380 --> 00:54:23.160 align:middle line:90%
all combined.

00:54:23.160 --> 00:54:24.930 align:middle line:90%
So how many we can combine?

00:54:24.930 --> 00:54:26.970 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, a
really good question.

00:54:26.970 --> 00:54:30.177 align:middle line:84%
Technically, as many as
you want, but at some point

00:54:30.177 --> 00:54:32.760 align:middle line:84%
your code is going to start to
look really, really bad, right?

00:54:32.760 --> 00:54:35.302 align:middle line:84%
Because the line of code is
going to get really, really long.

00:54:35.302 --> 00:54:38.130 align:middle line:84%
It's eventually going to maybe
wrap around again and again.

00:54:38.130 --> 00:54:41.220 align:middle line:84%
So at some point, you just kind of
say like, uh-uh, that's too many.

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:44.340 align:middle line:84%
And you start breaking it up
into multiple lines like I did.

00:54:44.340 --> 00:54:47.518 align:middle line:84%
Maybe reassigning the value
to the variable as needed.

00:54:47.518 --> 00:54:49.060 align:middle line:90%
And this is actually a good question.

00:54:49.060 --> 00:54:52.830 align:middle line:84%
If I can pivot, [INAUDIBLE] off your
question, I mean, what do people think?

00:54:52.830 --> 00:54:55.770 align:middle line:84%
If we could go ahead and put
everyone's hands down for a moment.

00:54:55.770 --> 00:54:57.690 align:middle line:90%
Let me ask this--

00:54:57.690 --> 00:55:03.450 align:middle line:84%
is the way I've done this now,
with strip and title and input

00:55:03.450 --> 00:55:04.800 align:middle line:90%
all in the same line--

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:07.290 align:middle line:90%
better than my previous approach?

00:55:07.290 --> 00:55:10.830 align:middle line:84%
In Zoom you can use the
Yes icon or the No icon.

00:55:10.830 --> 00:55:14.370 align:middle line:84%
If you think this version
is better, say Yes.

00:55:14.370 --> 00:55:19.060 align:middle line:84%
If you think this previous
version was better, for instance,

00:55:19.060 --> 00:55:23.310 align:middle line:84%
this one here where we had
everything broken out, say No.

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:26.610 align:middle line:90%
And then we'll see why in just a moment.

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:30.000 align:middle line:84%
I proposed earlier that
reasonable people can disagree

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:32.730 align:middle line:90%
and that's absolutely the case.

00:55:32.730 --> 00:55:35.520 align:middle line:84%
Doing it one way or the
other isn't necessarily best,

00:55:35.520 --> 00:55:37.800 align:middle line:90%
at least if you can justify it.

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:41.280 align:middle line:84%
Let me go back to the
most recent version here.

00:55:41.280 --> 00:55:45.360 align:middle line:84%
All right so we're seeing a
lot of Yeses and a lot of Nos.

00:55:45.360 --> 00:55:48.900 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we go ahead and call
on one of the Yeses, if we could.

00:55:48.900 --> 00:55:52.710 align:middle line:84%
Someone who's voting Yes, why do you
think the current version of this code

00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:57.330 align:middle line:84%
is indeed better than the previous
longer version of the code?

00:55:57.330 --> 00:55:59.110 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I think it's more readable.

00:55:59.110 --> 00:56:03.030 align:middle line:90%
So I can say, this is the name variable.

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:07.360 align:middle line:84%
It gets some input and then remove
the space and give it a title.

00:56:07.360 --> 00:56:08.320 align:middle line:90%
And there you go.

00:56:08.320 --> 00:56:09.600 align:middle line:90%
You have a hello, name.

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:10.860 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, I think
that's pretty reasonable.

00:56:10.860 --> 00:56:12.660 align:middle line:84%
It's very readable, at least
if you're in the habit,

00:56:12.660 --> 00:56:14.660 align:middle line:84%
as you are in English,
of reading left to right.

00:56:14.660 --> 00:56:16.990 align:middle line:84%
It just kind of flows very
naturally as a result.

00:56:16.990 --> 00:56:18.943 align:middle line:90%
The lines is not really that long.

00:56:18.943 --> 00:56:20.860 align:middle line:84%
It's certainly fitting
nicely onto the screen.

00:56:20.860 --> 00:56:21.870 align:middle line:90%
So I think that's a good argument.

00:56:21.870 --> 00:56:23.310 align:middle line:90%
How about a counterpoint, though?

00:56:23.310 --> 00:56:27.810 align:middle line:84%
Someone who voted No, if we could call
on someone who thinks this is worse.

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:31.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because it's
not readable at all.

00:56:31.410 --> 00:56:37.300 align:middle line:90%
It seems like it's a very long line.

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:39.170 align:middle line:90%
So I think it's better to separate.

00:56:39.170 --> 00:56:39.920 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: Yeah.

00:56:39.920 --> 00:56:41.580 align:middle line:90%
I think that's persuasive too, right?

00:56:41.580 --> 00:56:43.050 align:middle line:90%
It's getting a little longer.

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:46.890 align:middle line:84%
And even though my sentence here--
what's your name-- is relatively short,

00:56:46.890 --> 00:56:49.530 align:middle line:84%
you could imagine that this
could get even uglier quickly

00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:52.350 align:middle line:84%
if I were asking a longer
question of the user, that's

00:56:52.350 --> 00:56:56.070 align:middle line:84%
going to make this line of code even
longer and therefore less readable.

00:56:56.070 --> 00:56:59.970 align:middle line:84%
It might be less obvious to me or my
colleagues that I am calling strip

00:56:59.970 --> 00:57:01.530 align:middle line:90%
or that I am calling title.

00:57:01.530 --> 00:57:03.870 align:middle line:84%
It might be kind of an
unexpected surprise.

00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:05.460 align:middle line:90%
So I think that's reasonable too.

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:07.570 align:middle line:90%
In short, there is no right answer here.

00:57:07.570 --> 00:57:10.680 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, part of the process
of getting better at programming

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:14.168 align:middle line:84%
is getting your own sense of
style or working for a company

00:57:14.168 --> 00:57:16.710 align:middle line:84%
where they might prescribe which
way is better than the other

00:57:16.710 --> 00:57:18.930 align:middle line:84%
because they just want
everyone doing the same thing,

00:57:18.930 --> 00:57:22.080 align:middle line:84%
even though reasonable
people might disagree.

00:57:22.080 --> 00:57:24.750 align:middle line:84%
Ultimately though, so
long as you have what's

00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:28.710 align:middle line:84%
a pretty good argument in favor of
one way or the other, like ultimately,

00:57:28.710 --> 00:57:29.820 align:middle line:90%
that's what's important.

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:32.160 align:middle line:84%
If you're just doing things because you
don't really know which one is better,

00:57:32.160 --> 00:57:33.180 align:middle line:90%
that's not great.

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:35.460 align:middle line:84%
But if and when you
start to acquire opinions

00:57:35.460 --> 00:57:38.610 align:middle line:84%
and if your boss, if your teacher,
if your colleague, your friend,

00:57:38.610 --> 00:57:41.312 align:middle line:84%
can challenge you and say, wait,
why did you do it like this?

00:57:41.312 --> 00:57:43.770 align:middle line:84%
They might not agree with you,
but at least have an answer,

00:57:43.770 --> 00:57:47.010 align:middle line:84%
and that should be sufficiently
persuasive in general.

00:57:47.010 --> 00:57:49.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, strings come with a
whole bunch of other methods

00:57:49.230 --> 00:57:53.310 align:middle line:84%
as well, among which is one called
split, which can, as the name suggests,

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:57.030 align:middle line:84%
split a string into multiple
smaller substrings, so to speak.

00:57:57.030 --> 00:57:59.040 align:middle line:84%
For instance, if the
human here is in the habit

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:02.130 align:middle line:84%
of typing in their first name, then
a space, and then their last name,

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:05.070 align:middle line:84%
and you want to go ahead and
greet them only by first name,

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:07.140 align:middle line:84%
well we could actually
leverage that single space

00:58:07.140 --> 00:58:09.930 align:middle line:84%
between the first name and
last name and split that string

00:58:09.930 --> 00:58:11.760 align:middle line:90%
into two smaller substrings.

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:12.820 align:middle line:90%
How can we do this?

00:58:12.820 --> 00:58:14.820 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me go ahead and
in between these lines

00:58:14.820 --> 00:58:18.210 align:middle line:84%
proactively comment that
we're about to split user's

00:58:18.210 --> 00:58:22.230 align:middle line:90%
name into first name and last name.

00:58:22.230 --> 00:58:25.920 align:middle line:84%
And then let's go ahead and take that
name variable, which currently contains

00:58:25.920 --> 00:58:28.590 align:middle line:84%
something like, presumably,
David, space, Malan,

00:58:28.590 --> 00:58:32.130 align:middle line:84%
and let me go ahead and call
split and pass in as the argument

00:58:32.130 --> 00:58:35.820 align:middle line:84%
to split a single white space,
thereby indicating that I indeed

00:58:35.820 --> 00:58:37.750 align:middle line:90%
want to split on that character

00:58:37.750 --> 00:58:41.363 align:middle line:84%
Now it turns out split is going to
return a sequence of values, ideally

00:58:41.363 --> 00:58:42.780 align:middle line:90%
a first name and then a last name.

00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:45.330 align:middle line:84%
And we can actually, in Python,
assign both of those values

00:58:45.330 --> 00:58:47.940 align:middle line:84%
from that sequence at
once to some variables.

00:58:47.940 --> 00:58:51.360 align:middle line:84%
For instance, first comma
last equals, and that's

00:58:51.360 --> 00:58:53.220 align:middle line:84%
going to have the effect
from right to left

00:58:53.220 --> 00:58:56.760 align:middle line:84%
of putting the first such value in the
first variable, the second such value

00:58:56.760 --> 00:58:57.880 align:middle line:90%
in the second variable.

00:58:57.880 --> 00:59:01.350 align:middle line:84%
So now on my last line of
code, I can go in and say hello

00:59:01.350 --> 00:59:03.900 align:middle line:84%
not to the full name,
something like David Malan,

00:59:03.900 --> 00:59:06.540 align:middle line:90%
I can just say Hello, first.

00:59:06.540 --> 00:59:10.200 align:middle line:84%
All right, let's go ahead and clear my
terminal window, run Python of Hello.py

00:59:10.200 --> 00:59:10.807 align:middle line:90%
and hit Enter.

00:59:10.807 --> 00:59:13.890 align:middle line:84%
I won't bother with any leading white
space this time, but let me go ahead

00:59:13.890 --> 00:59:19.290 align:middle line:84%
and type in David space Malan, and
crossing my fingers as usual, Hello,

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:21.720 align:middle line:90%
David is what we now see.

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:25.530 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we've seen so much,
so many examples thus far involving

00:59:25.530 --> 00:59:29.430 align:middle line:84%
strings, but certainly programs
and programming languages can

00:59:29.430 --> 00:59:32.080 align:middle line:90%
manipulate other types of data as well.

00:59:32.080 --> 00:59:36.000 align:middle line:84%
Let's go ahead and transition then
to another very common type of data

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:38.610 align:middle line:84%
in Python, in programming
more generally, namely

00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:41.160 align:middle line:84%
integers, otherwise
known in Python as INT--

00:59:41.160 --> 00:59:46.080 align:middle line:84%
I-N-T. So just as STR, S-T-R is
short for string, so is INT in Python

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:47.210 align:middle line:90%
short for integer.

00:59:47.210 --> 00:59:48.210 align:middle line:90%
Well, what's an integer?

00:59:48.210 --> 00:59:51.390 align:middle line:84%
Well just like in math it's
a number like negative 2,

00:59:51.390 --> 00:59:55.605 align:middle line:84%
negative 1, 0, 1, 2, and all the
way toward negative infinity, all

00:59:55.605 --> 00:59:56.980 align:middle line:90%
the way toward positive infinity.

00:59:56.980 --> 00:59:59.040 align:middle line:84%
But there's no decimal
point in an integer.

00:59:59.040 --> 01:00:04.260 align:middle line:84%
It's just a number like negative
2, negative 1, 0, 1, and 2 onward.

01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:05.160 align:middle line:90%
That's an INT.

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:09.360 align:middle line:84%
Of course, in the world of mathematics,
there's lots of symbols that we use.

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:12.300 align:middle line:84%
And we've seen + before, although
we used it for a different purpose.

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:15.030 align:middle line:84%
But Python supports
these symbols and more.

01:00:15.030 --> 01:00:18.090 align:middle line:84%
And Python allows you to
add numbers together +,

01:00:18.090 --> 01:00:21.580 align:middle line:84%
subtract numbers, multiply
numbers, divide numbers.

01:00:21.580 --> 01:00:25.380 align:middle line:84%
And the only one here that might look a
little strange to people or unfamiliar

01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:29.310 align:middle line:84%
is this percent sign, but it doesn't
mean percent in this context.

01:00:29.310 --> 01:00:32.400 align:middle line:84%
If you use a single percent
sign in a Python program,

01:00:32.400 --> 01:00:34.680 align:middle line:84%
that's actually the
so-called modulo operator,

01:00:34.680 --> 01:00:38.400 align:middle line:84%
the operator that allows you to
take the remainder after dividing

01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:40.210 align:middle line:90%
one number by another.

01:00:40.210 --> 01:00:43.230 align:middle line:84%
So we'll see examples of that before
long, but the first four of these

01:00:43.230 --> 01:00:45.570 align:middle line:90%
are perhaps quite, quite familiar.

01:00:45.570 --> 01:00:49.240 align:middle line:84%
Well it turns out that in
Python you cannot necessarily--

01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:54.480 align:middle line:84%
you don't necessarily have to keep
writing code in a file like Hello.py

01:00:54.480 --> 01:00:56.700 align:middle line:84%
and then running it
in a terminal window.

01:00:56.700 --> 01:00:59.610 align:middle line:84%
One of the features that
many people like about Python

01:00:59.610 --> 01:01:02.250 align:middle line:84%
is that it supports this
so-called interactive mode.

01:01:02.250 --> 01:01:06.240 align:middle line:84%
Like you can start writing Python
code and immediately execute

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:08.965 align:middle line:84%
each of those lines
interactively, especially

01:01:08.965 --> 01:01:11.340 align:middle line:84%
if you don't care about saving
all of your lines of code.

01:01:11.340 --> 01:01:14.590 align:middle line:84%
You just want to execute code
and get back some answers.

01:01:14.590 --> 01:01:18.400 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, let me go back to VS
Code here and let me close Hello.py

01:01:18.400 --> 01:01:22.050 align:middle line:84%
and let me click on the little triangle
over here in my terminal window just

01:01:22.050 --> 01:01:24.880 align:middle line:84%
to make it much bigger just
temporarily for a moment.

01:01:24.880 --> 01:01:30.450 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not creating any .py file now,
I'm just going to run Python by itself

01:01:30.450 --> 01:01:31.410 align:middle line:90%
at my prompt.

01:01:31.410 --> 01:01:35.100 align:middle line:84%
And you'll see when I do this, I
get some cryptic looking output

01:01:35.100 --> 01:01:38.890 align:middle line:84%
and the date and time at which the
program was last updated, and so forth.

01:01:38.890 --> 01:01:43.710 align:middle line:84%
But I ultimately get three
triple brackets like this.

01:01:43.710 --> 01:01:46.740 align:middle line:90%
Is the interactive mode for Python.

01:01:46.740 --> 01:01:52.050 align:middle line:84%
So I'm running the Python interpreter
and any time I type a line of code

01:01:52.050 --> 01:01:54.570 align:middle line:84%
in the interpreter, it's going
to execute it immediately.

01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:56.942 align:middle line:84%
I don't have to keep running
Python again and again.

01:01:56.942 --> 01:01:58.650 align:middle line:84%
It's as though in the
human world, if you

01:01:58.650 --> 01:02:01.635 align:middle line:84%
were standing next to a human
who speaks some other language,

01:02:01.635 --> 01:02:04.260 align:middle line:84%
and you're just having a conversation
with them back and forth,

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:07.030 align:middle line:84%
it's all happening-- the
translation-- immediately.

01:02:07.030 --> 01:02:08.940 align:middle line:90%
So what might I do in interactive mode?

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:11.760 align:middle line:84%
Well I could do something
like 1 + 1, Enter.

01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:13.380 align:middle line:90%
That's actually code, all right?

01:02:13.380 --> 01:02:16.590 align:middle line:84%
You might not think of it as code,
but if a bit of arithmetic and you

01:02:16.590 --> 01:02:21.060 align:middle line:84%
know numbers, and you know
+, that's valid Python code.

01:02:21.060 --> 01:02:23.537 align:middle line:84%
And you can use Python
really as a fancy calculator.

01:02:23.537 --> 01:02:24.870 align:middle line:90%
But I could do other things too.

01:02:24.870 --> 01:02:28.020 align:middle line:84%
If I want to print to
myself Hello, world,

01:02:28.020 --> 01:02:30.750 align:middle line:84%
I can also print out that
line of code there too--

01:02:30.750 --> 01:02:31.530 align:middle line:90%
Hello, world.

01:02:31.530 --> 01:02:33.600 align:middle line:84%
So it's interactive in
the sense that the moment

01:02:33.600 --> 01:02:36.960 align:middle line:84%
you execute a line of code,
boom, you see the result.

01:02:36.960 --> 01:02:39.450 align:middle line:84%
We're generally not going to
do that because at least when

01:02:39.450 --> 01:02:42.810 align:middle line:84%
teaching the language, we tend to
want to do things incrementally

01:02:42.810 --> 01:02:45.513 align:middle line:84%
and we want you to be able to
see where it is we came from.

01:02:45.513 --> 01:02:48.180 align:middle line:84%
And we want to be able to try
things again and again, especially

01:02:48.180 --> 01:02:49.140 align:middle line:90%
if we make mistakes.

01:02:49.140 --> 01:02:51.690 align:middle line:84%
But know that this is
indeed a feature of Python,

01:02:51.690 --> 01:02:54.250 align:middle line:90%
this so-called interactive mode.

01:02:54.250 --> 01:02:57.015 align:middle line:84%
But let's focus for a moment now,
not just on that interactivity,

01:02:57.015 --> 01:02:58.890 align:middle line:84%
but really on the fact
that Python apparently

01:02:58.890 --> 01:03:03.310 align:middle line:84%
supports integers and mathematics
and some of those basic operations.

01:03:03.310 --> 01:03:06.430 align:middle line:84%
And let's see if we can't make
maybe our own little calculator.

01:03:06.430 --> 01:03:08.820 align:middle line:84%
So let me go ahead and
open up VS Code again,

01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:11.440 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to shrink
down my terminal window,

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:14.610 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to create a new
file called Calculator.py.

01:03:14.610 --> 01:03:17.950 align:middle line:84%
So to do that recall, I
can type Code down here,

01:03:17.950 --> 01:03:21.540 align:middle line:84%
and the name of the file I
want to create, .py, Enter.

01:03:21.540 --> 01:03:23.550 align:middle line:90%
That gives me a new tab up top.

01:03:23.550 --> 01:03:25.620 align:middle line:90%
So I have already closed Hello.py.

01:03:25.620 --> 01:03:27.150 align:middle line:90%
I'm now in Calculator.py.

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:30.900 align:middle line:84%
And let's just make a simple calculator
that does some addition for me.

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:33.690 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to do it in a file
so that we can iterate on this

01:03:33.690 --> 01:03:36.607 align:middle line:84%
and make changes for better
or for worse over time.

01:03:36.607 --> 01:03:38.940 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and first
declare a couple of variables.

01:03:38.940 --> 01:03:42.840 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to do the mathematical
thing of calling my first variable x,

01:03:42.840 --> 01:03:45.840 align:middle line:84%
my second variable y, and
then I'm going to give myself

01:03:45.840 --> 01:03:48.720 align:middle line:90%
a third variable z = x + y.

01:03:48.720 --> 01:03:50.970 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to go
ahead and print out z.

01:03:50.970 --> 01:03:54.858 align:middle line:84%
Now this program, admittedly not
very exciting or interesting,

01:03:54.858 --> 01:03:57.900 align:middle line:84%
in fact, it's a little less interesting
than printing stuff on the screen

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:01.110 align:middle line:84%
like before with strings,
but we'll build on this

01:04:01.110 --> 01:04:04.030 align:middle line:84%
and see what other features exist
in Python that we can leverage.

01:04:04.030 --> 01:04:07.410 align:middle line:84%
So hopefully, if Python knows
it's math as well as I do,

01:04:07.410 --> 01:04:13.140 align:middle line:84%
when I run Python of Calculator.py,
I should see hopefully that 1 + 2

01:04:13.140 --> 01:04:15.600 align:middle line:90%
= indeed 3.

01:04:15.600 --> 01:04:18.510 align:middle line:84%
All right, so not that surprising
and not that interesting.

01:04:18.510 --> 01:04:20.730 align:middle line:84%
And honestly this isn't
the most useful program

01:04:20.730 --> 01:04:24.840 align:middle line:84%
because it's always going
to calculate 1 + 2 = 3.

01:04:24.840 --> 01:04:28.170 align:middle line:84%
Let's at least make this program,
say, a little more interactive, right?

01:04:28.170 --> 01:04:31.590 align:middle line:84%
We already know from previous examples
how we can get input from the user.

01:04:31.590 --> 01:04:33.540 align:middle line:90%
Let's bring back that input function.

01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:37.380 align:middle line:84%
And let's do this, let me go ahead
now and at the top of my code,

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:40.650 align:middle line:84%
let's change x to not
be the number 1 always,

01:04:40.650 --> 01:04:42.510 align:middle line:84%
let's change it to be
whatever the return

01:04:42.510 --> 01:04:45.690 align:middle line:90%
value is of asking the user for x.

01:04:45.690 --> 01:04:48.730 align:middle line:84%
And I can use any English or
human language I want here.

01:04:48.730 --> 01:04:49.890 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to say, what's x?

01:04:49.890 --> 01:04:52.410 align:middle line:84%
Just like I asked
before, what's your name?

01:04:52.410 --> 01:04:54.180 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to do
the same thing for y.

01:04:54.180 --> 01:04:57.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use Input again, but
this time change the question to be,

01:04:57.330 --> 01:04:58.728 align:middle line:90%
what's y?

01:04:58.728 --> 01:05:01.770 align:middle line:84%
All right, at this point, I think I'm
going to leave the rest of the code

01:05:01.770 --> 01:05:02.730 align:middle line:90%
the same.

01:05:02.730 --> 01:05:06.450 align:middle line:84%
Z = x + y And then
print z but what's nice

01:05:06.450 --> 01:05:10.140 align:middle line:84%
now is that I think I have a
nice interactive calculator.

01:05:10.140 --> 01:05:12.510 align:middle line:84%
Right, now it's not going
to do 1 + 2 all the time.

01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:16.330 align:middle line:84%
It's going to do whatever the user
types + whatever the user types.

01:05:16.330 --> 01:05:17.530 align:middle line:90%
So let's try this.

01:05:17.530 --> 01:05:19.080 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead and run that program.

01:05:19.080 --> 01:05:20.110 align:middle line:90%
All right, let's do it.

01:05:20.110 --> 01:05:26.670 align:middle line:84%
1 is going to be x, 2 is going to be y,
and of course, everyone in agreement, 1

01:05:26.670 --> 01:05:30.762 align:middle line:90%
+ 2 = thr--

01:05:30.762 --> 01:05:31.980 align:middle line:90%
huh.

01:05:31.980 --> 01:05:36.040 align:middle line:90%
What's going on there?

01:05:36.040 --> 01:05:40.720 align:middle line:84%
Either your math class misled
you or I have misled you.

01:05:40.720 --> 01:05:43.000 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we call on
someone here to see if you

01:05:43.000 --> 01:05:46.900 align:middle line:84%
can't help us reason through what
the bug is, what's the mistake?

01:05:46.900 --> 01:05:48.970 align:middle line:90%
Anjali, if I'm saying it right.

01:05:48.970 --> 01:05:52.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think the issue is that
it's concatenating strings because you

01:05:52.960 --> 01:05:54.935 align:middle line:90%
use the + operator instead of adding.

01:05:54.935 --> 01:05:55.810 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: Perfect.

01:05:55.810 --> 01:05:57.040 align:middle line:90%
So perfect intuition.

01:05:57.040 --> 01:06:01.060 align:middle line:84%
We've seen that + is used a little
differently in the context of strings

01:06:01.060 --> 01:06:03.940 align:middle line:84%
because it concatenates, that
is, it joins the two strings,

01:06:03.940 --> 01:06:06.100 align:middle line:84%
and that seems to Indeed
be what's happening here,

01:06:06.100 --> 01:06:07.990 align:middle line:90%
even though the user typed a number.

01:06:07.990 --> 01:06:09.910 align:middle line:84%
But the interesting
thing here is that, when

01:06:09.910 --> 01:06:13.660 align:middle line:84%
you get user input, because they're
using a keyboard on their Mac

01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:16.900 align:middle line:84%
or PC or their phone, it
is always going to be text.

01:06:16.900 --> 01:06:19.030 align:middle line:84%
It might look like a
number, but by default,

01:06:19.030 --> 01:06:21.850 align:middle line:84%
it's coming from the
keyboard as a string--

01:06:21.850 --> 01:06:23.560 align:middle line:90%
that is, as text.

01:06:23.560 --> 01:06:27.760 align:middle line:84%
And so, how do we go about
resolving this if ultimately we

01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:30.190 align:middle line:84%
don't want to treat
those inputs as strings,

01:06:30.190 --> 01:06:32.110 align:middle line:90%
we want to treat them as actual numbers?

01:06:32.110 --> 01:06:35.050 align:middle line:84%
Well we need another function
and it turns out in Python

01:06:35.050 --> 01:06:39.610 align:middle line:84%
that you can convert sometimes from one
type of data to another type of data,

01:06:39.610 --> 01:06:43.880 align:middle line:84%
for instance, from string to INT
by doing something like this.

01:06:43.880 --> 01:06:46.690 align:middle line:84%
Let me go back into my
code and let me change

01:06:46.690 --> 01:06:51.730 align:middle line:84%
x before adding it to y to be
whatever the integer version of x

01:06:51.730 --> 01:06:56.030 align:middle line:84%
is, + whatever the
integer version of y is.

01:06:56.030 --> 01:06:59.950 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that INT is not
only a type of data in Python,

01:06:59.950 --> 01:07:02.590 align:middle line:84%
it's also a function,
and it's a function

01:07:02.590 --> 01:07:05.410 align:middle line:84%
that if you pass in an
input, like a string,

01:07:05.410 --> 01:07:09.790 align:middle line:84%
so long as that string looks
like a number like 1 or like 2,

01:07:09.790 --> 01:07:13.450 align:middle line:84%
it will convert it to an actual number
that you can perform mathematics

01:07:13.450 --> 01:07:14.510 align:middle line:90%
on instead.

01:07:14.510 --> 01:07:18.340 align:middle line:84%
So if I now go back to my
terminal window and run Python--

01:07:18.340 --> 01:07:19.870 align:middle line:90%
and let me show you another trick.

01:07:19.870 --> 01:07:21.580 align:middle line:90%
"Calculator" is kind of a long word.

01:07:21.580 --> 01:07:22.930 align:middle line:90%
It's a little tedious to type.

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:26.470 align:middle line:84%
Notice what I can do in my
terminal window, in a command line

01:07:26.470 --> 01:07:27.460 align:middle line:90%
interface in general.

01:07:27.460 --> 01:07:31.300 align:middle line:84%
If I start typing C-A-L for
calculator, I can actually

01:07:31.300 --> 01:07:33.700 align:middle line:90%
hit Tab to finish my thought.

01:07:33.700 --> 01:07:36.550 align:middle line:84%
So auto-complete is possible
in a terminal window like this.

01:07:36.550 --> 01:07:39.663 align:middle line:84%
Type the first letter or few
letters and then, boom, with Tab,

01:07:39.663 --> 01:07:41.080 align:middle line:90%
it'll finish your thought for you.

01:07:41.080 --> 01:07:44.770 align:middle line:84%
Or you can go back in your history
like I did with the Up and Down Arrows.

01:07:44.770 --> 01:07:46.570 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead and execute this.

01:07:46.570 --> 01:07:47.270 align:middle line:90%
What's x?

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:47.770 align:middle line:90%
1.

01:07:47.770 --> 01:07:48.290 align:middle line:90%
What's x?

01:07:48.290 --> 01:07:48.790 align:middle line:90%
2.

01:07:48.790 --> 01:07:49.880 align:middle line:90%
And there we go.

01:07:49.880 --> 01:07:53.050 align:middle line:84%
Now we have a general purpose
calculator that's going to support

01:07:53.050 --> 01:07:57.280 align:middle line:84%
not just the addition of
1 and 2, but now any two

01:07:57.280 --> 01:07:58.882 align:middle line:90%
integers that the user types.

01:07:58.882 --> 01:08:00.340 align:middle line:90%
And let me now improve this, right?

01:08:00.340 --> 01:08:03.190 align:middle line:84%
We've seen how we can
make improvements to code

01:08:03.190 --> 01:08:05.650 align:middle line:84%
and I don't know if it's going
to necessarily be better,

01:08:05.650 --> 01:08:07.000 align:middle line:90%
but let's try this.

01:08:07.000 --> 01:08:09.730 align:middle line:90%
Do I really need the z variable?

01:08:09.730 --> 01:08:13.630 align:middle line:84%
It's worth noting that I'm
creating a variable called z,

01:08:13.630 --> 01:08:16.720 align:middle line:84%
and then I'm immediately using
it on the next line of code.

01:08:16.720 --> 01:08:20.770 align:middle line:84%
Now that's not that compelling,
because if you're creating a variable

01:08:20.770 --> 01:08:23.470 align:middle line:84%
and then immediately using
it, but never again using it,

01:08:23.470 --> 01:08:26.890 align:middle line:84%
did you really need to take the
time to introduce another symbol

01:08:26.890 --> 01:08:30.279 align:middle line:84%
and another variable just to
use it once and only once?

01:08:30.279 --> 01:08:31.090 align:middle line:90%
Well, maybe not.

01:08:31.090 --> 01:08:33.729 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we don't really
need z in this way.

01:08:33.729 --> 01:08:38.319 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I should go and
do something like this.

01:08:38.319 --> 01:08:41.140 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I should get rid of z here.

01:08:41.140 --> 01:08:45.729 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I should change this
to be INT up here, change

01:08:45.729 --> 01:08:50.260 align:middle line:84%
this to be INT up here, doing something
that's pretty interesting now.

01:08:50.260 --> 01:08:53.680 align:middle line:84%
Even though it's a bit of
new syntax, notice that you

01:08:53.680 --> 01:08:56.380 align:middle line:90%
can nest functions, so to speak.

01:08:56.380 --> 01:09:01.330 align:middle line:84%
You can put one function call
that is the use of a function

01:09:01.330 --> 01:09:04.540 align:middle line:84%
inside of the use of another
function so that the return

01:09:04.540 --> 01:09:09.040 align:middle line:84%
value of the inner function
becomes the argument to

01:09:09.040 --> 01:09:11.410 align:middle line:90%
or the input to the outer function.

01:09:11.410 --> 01:09:14.510 align:middle line:84%
Just like in math, if you have
parentheses, parentheses, parentheses,

01:09:14.510 --> 01:09:16.218 align:middle line:84%
your teacher probably
taught you to focus

01:09:16.218 --> 01:09:18.430 align:middle line:84%
on what's inside the
innermost parentheses first

01:09:18.430 --> 01:09:19.603 align:middle line:90%
and then work your way out.

01:09:19.603 --> 01:09:20.770 align:middle line:90%
Same thing with programming.

01:09:20.770 --> 01:09:22.040 align:middle line:90%
That's what's Python is going to do.

01:09:22.040 --> 01:09:24.729 align:middle line:84%
It's going to look at what's
inside of the parentheses first,

01:09:24.729 --> 01:09:26.920 align:middle line:84%
it's going to get the
answer, and then it's

01:09:26.920 --> 01:09:30.710 align:middle line:84%
going to pass the return value
to the outermost function.

01:09:30.710 --> 01:09:34.600 align:middle line:84%
So what happens on line 1 now is that
the input function gets called first,

01:09:34.600 --> 01:09:37.990 align:middle line:84%
then the result of
that, quote unquote one

01:09:37.990 --> 01:09:42.670 align:middle line:84%
becomes the input to the INT
function, and same on line 2.

01:09:42.670 --> 01:09:47.300 align:middle line:84%
The output of what's y becomes
the input to this INT function.

01:09:47.300 --> 01:09:49.479 align:middle line:90%
And now there is no z.

01:09:49.479 --> 01:09:52.210 align:middle line:90%
I could just do print x + y.

01:09:52.210 --> 01:09:56.410 align:middle line:84%
And because I've taken the time
to convert each of those strings

01:09:56.410 --> 01:09:58.720 align:middle line:90%
to an integer, I think we're OK.

01:09:58.720 --> 01:10:01.570 align:middle line:84%
So let me try this, Python
of Calculator.py, Enter.

01:10:01.570 --> 01:10:05.170 align:middle line:90%
1 and 2, and we're still getting 3.

01:10:05.170 --> 01:10:09.130 align:middle line:84%
Not 12, or not 12, 1,2,
we're indeed getting 3.

01:10:09.130 --> 01:10:11.350 align:middle line:84%
And we've additionally
gotten rid of the variable

01:10:11.350 --> 01:10:13.900 align:middle line:84%
because we didn't necessarily
need it, it seems,

01:10:13.900 --> 01:10:15.490 align:middle line:90%
especially if only using it once.

01:10:15.490 --> 01:10:18.280 align:middle line:84%
Well here too, let me put everyone's
hands down for just a moment

01:10:18.280 --> 01:10:19.930 align:middle line:90%
and let me ask as before--

01:10:19.930 --> 01:10:26.530 align:middle line:84%
this version now which uses INT
around the invocations of input,

01:10:26.530 --> 01:10:30.640 align:middle line:84%
and does not use z, is this
better than the previous version?

01:10:30.640 --> 01:10:32.560 align:middle line:90%
If you want to vote Yes, go ahead.

01:10:32.560 --> 01:10:35.270 align:middle line:90%
Or if you prefer the old way, vote No.

01:10:35.270 --> 01:10:40.455 align:middle line:84%
The old way, I'll undo all of this
as we vote, instead looked like this.

01:10:40.455 --> 01:10:43.510 align:middle line:90%


01:10:43.510 --> 01:10:45.850 align:middle line:84%
All right, and let me go back
to now the newest version.

01:10:45.850 --> 01:10:48.040 align:middle line:84%
Let's take a hand of
the Yeses, someone who

01:10:48.040 --> 01:10:51.430 align:middle line:90%
thinks this latest version is better.

01:10:51.430 --> 01:10:57.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think this way is better
because it allows us to immediately see

01:10:57.130 --> 01:11:01.840 align:middle line:84%
what the x and y variables
are with integers

01:11:01.840 --> 01:11:04.660 align:middle line:90%
and so we know what to expect from them.

01:11:04.660 --> 01:11:09.250 align:middle line:84%
And also the print
argument is more intuitive.

01:11:09.250 --> 01:11:12.330 align:middle line:90%
We avoid too much clutter in the code.

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:14.330 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: I think those
are all good reasons.

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:15.370 align:middle line:90%
It's nice and succinct.

01:11:15.370 --> 01:11:17.650 align:middle line:90%
The lines of code are not very long.

01:11:17.650 --> 01:11:20.530 align:middle line:84%
I don't need to know what z
is because it doesn't exist.

01:11:20.530 --> 01:11:21.820 align:middle line:90%
It just sees print x + y.

01:11:21.820 --> 01:11:22.390 align:middle line:90%
I like that.

01:11:22.390 --> 01:11:25.990 align:middle line:84%
But someone who prefers the
older way where we did have z

01:11:25.990 --> 01:11:30.970 align:middle line:84%
and we more explicitly passed individual
variables to the INT function.

01:11:30.970 --> 01:11:32.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, hi.

01:11:32.050 --> 01:11:36.100 align:middle line:84%
I think that the earlier
version is better because when--

01:11:36.100 --> 01:11:39.370 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if user input
something else other than,

01:11:39.370 --> 01:11:43.570 align:middle line:84%
let's say, I mean, let's
say, they type 1 and 2, like,

01:11:43.570 --> 01:11:46.780 align:middle line:90%
so it will be easier to debug.

01:11:46.780 --> 01:11:49.807 align:middle line:84%
DAVID MALAN: This version or-- this
version here or the old version?

01:11:49.807 --> 01:11:50.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The old version.

01:11:50.890 --> 01:11:52.098 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: OK, that's fair.

01:11:52.098 --> 01:11:57.190 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, I'm being very careful
today, as best I can, not to mess up.

01:11:57.190 --> 01:12:01.050 align:middle line:84%
I have thus far only inputted
integers when I'm expecting integers.

01:12:01.050 --> 01:12:02.800 align:middle line:84%
And Raoul's actually
pointing to something

01:12:02.800 --> 01:12:05.980 align:middle line:84%
we'll come back to in the coming weeks,
how do we actually handle errors?

01:12:05.980 --> 01:12:09.770 align:middle line:84%
What if the user doesn't type in the
number 1 or the number 2, or a number

01:12:09.770 --> 01:12:10.270 align:middle line:90%
at all?

01:12:10.270 --> 01:12:13.390 align:middle line:84%
What if they type in a
word like cat, C-A-T?

01:12:13.390 --> 01:12:16.150 align:middle line:84%
That's not a number, and I bet I
can't convert it to an integer.

01:12:16.150 --> 01:12:18.110 align:middle line:84%
But for today, I'm not
going to focus on that.

01:12:18.110 --> 01:12:20.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to hope
that the user cooperates.

01:12:20.110 --> 01:12:21.740 align:middle line:90%
But that's not going to be the case.

01:12:21.740 --> 01:12:24.280 align:middle line:84%
And so perhaps one way would
set us up for more success

01:12:24.280 --> 01:12:26.020 align:middle line:90%
when it comes to handling those errors.

01:12:26.020 --> 01:12:28.495 align:middle line:84%
Now for today's purposes,
which is better?

01:12:28.495 --> 01:12:29.650 align:middle line:90%
Eh, I mean, I like both.

01:12:29.650 --> 01:12:32.440 align:middle line:84%
And I think both of you made very
valid arguments in there too,

01:12:32.440 --> 01:12:36.250 align:middle line:84%
so long as you have a justification
that feels pretty reasonable.

01:12:36.250 --> 01:12:37.930 align:middle line:90%
I mean, that's what ultimately matters.

01:12:37.930 --> 01:12:41.500 align:middle line:84%
But acquiring, again, a
sense of the trade-offs here.

01:12:41.500 --> 01:12:43.180 align:middle line:90%
Well, is this way better?

01:12:43.180 --> 01:12:44.885 align:middle line:90%
If so, why or why not?

01:12:44.885 --> 01:12:46.760 align:middle line:84%
Just understanding what
those trade-offs are.

01:12:46.760 --> 01:12:51.460 align:middle line:84%
But generally speaking, prioritizing
readability is a very good thing.

01:12:51.460 --> 01:12:54.740 align:middle line:84%
Making your code readable for
someone else is a very good thing

01:12:54.740 --> 01:12:57.608 align:middle line:84%
and very good for you too so that
when you wake up the next morning,

01:12:57.608 --> 01:12:59.650 align:middle line:84%
or you come back the next
week, or the next year,

01:12:59.650 --> 01:13:02.290 align:middle line:84%
you too can read your own
code without having to waste

01:13:02.290 --> 01:13:04.270 align:middle line:90%
time trying to remember what you did.

01:13:04.270 --> 01:13:08.500 align:middle line:84%
And simplicity tends to be a good
thing too-- keeping your code simple.

01:13:08.500 --> 01:13:10.960 align:middle line:84%
So as you get more
comfortable with programming,

01:13:10.960 --> 01:13:15.250 align:middle line:84%
you might be tempted to try to combine
an entire program into one long line.

01:13:15.250 --> 01:13:17.740 align:middle line:90%
For instance, let me do just that.

01:13:17.740 --> 01:13:20.680 align:middle line:84%
Technically speaking, we don't
really need x in a variable.

01:13:20.680 --> 01:13:22.480 align:middle line:90%
We don't really need y in a variable.

01:13:22.480 --> 01:13:24.460 align:middle line:90%
We could also do this--

01:13:24.460 --> 01:13:27.820 align:middle line:84%
I could just get rid
of x and y altogether.

01:13:27.820 --> 01:13:32.080 align:middle line:84%
I could then now eliminate that
and make it just one line of code.

01:13:32.080 --> 01:13:34.510 align:middle line:84%
OK, so on some sense, you might
be inclined to think, wow,

01:13:34.510 --> 01:13:35.740 align:middle line:90%
that's really nice.

01:13:35.740 --> 01:13:38.410 align:middle line:90%
You made it one simple line of code.

01:13:38.410 --> 01:13:40.520 align:middle line:84%
I would argue this
actually isn't that simple.

01:13:40.520 --> 01:13:43.180 align:middle line:84%
Now I think I'm starting
to nest too many things.

01:13:43.180 --> 01:13:46.330 align:middle line:84%
I have to think about
print and INT and input.

01:13:46.330 --> 01:13:49.150 align:middle line:84%
I then have to notice that, OK,
I've opened two parentheses,

01:13:49.150 --> 01:13:50.300 align:middle line:90%
I've closed two of them.

01:13:50.300 --> 01:13:51.040 align:middle line:90%
There's a +.

01:13:51.040 --> 01:13:53.680 align:middle line:84%
You're making me think too much
and any time you make me think,

01:13:53.680 --> 01:13:54.730 align:middle line:90%
you're wasting time.

01:13:54.730 --> 01:13:58.360 align:middle line:84%
And any time you complicate
the look of the code like this,

01:13:58.360 --> 01:14:01.300 align:middle line:84%
you're just going to increase
the probability of mistakes

01:14:01.300 --> 01:14:04.270 align:middle line:84%
and tactical mistakes or
logical errors in your code.

01:14:04.270 --> 01:14:07.810 align:middle line:84%
So if all the things we've done, this
is the only one that I would argue,

01:14:07.810 --> 01:14:10.870 align:middle line:84%
yes, it's one line and
it's nice and compact.

01:14:10.870 --> 01:14:12.340 align:middle line:90%
It's just not readable enough.

01:14:12.340 --> 01:14:15.850 align:middle line:84%
I would shy away from doing this,
especially since two of those function

01:14:15.850 --> 01:14:18.040 align:middle line:90%
calls are getting input from the user.

01:14:18.040 --> 01:14:20.410 align:middle line:84%
But there too, reasonable
people might disagree,

01:14:20.410 --> 01:14:23.830 align:middle line:84%
but that's the kind of visceral
reaction you should have sometimes when

01:14:23.830 --> 01:14:28.300 align:middle line:84%
code starts getting a little too
complicated, a little too clever,

01:14:28.300 --> 01:14:31.450 align:middle line:90%
perhaps, for its own good.

01:14:31.450 --> 01:14:34.270 align:middle line:84%
All right, well it's not just
integers we have access to.

01:14:34.270 --> 01:14:38.920 align:middle line:84%
Let me propose that we transition from
integers to one more data type here,

01:14:38.920 --> 01:14:40.360 align:middle line:90%
namely a float.

01:14:40.360 --> 01:14:43.390 align:middle line:84%
So again, a string is
a sequence of text.

01:14:43.390 --> 01:14:46.570 align:middle line:84%
An INT is an integer
like negative 1, 0 and 1.

01:14:46.570 --> 01:14:50.860 align:middle line:84%
A float is a number with a decimal
point, properly called a floating point

01:14:50.860 --> 01:14:51.470 align:middle line:90%
value.

01:14:51.470 --> 01:14:53.470 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of the
floating point as being

01:14:53.470 --> 01:14:56.740 align:middle line:84%
the decimal that might be over here or
over here with some number of digits

01:14:56.740 --> 01:14:57.820 align:middle line:90%
to the left or the right.

01:14:57.820 --> 01:15:02.000 align:middle line:84%
Mathematically, it's a real number, a
number that has a decimal point in it.

01:15:02.000 --> 01:15:04.750 align:middle line:84%
So that's a third type of
data that Python supports.

01:15:04.750 --> 01:15:07.270 align:middle line:84%
Right now our calculator
is somewhat naively

01:15:07.270 --> 01:15:09.820 align:middle line:84%
assuming that the user is only
going to type in integers,

01:15:09.820 --> 01:15:12.970 align:middle line:84%
but if I want to support
floating point values too,

01:15:12.970 --> 01:15:15.070 align:middle line:84%
I think I can just make
a couple of tweaks.

01:15:15.070 --> 01:15:17.270 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to go back to VS Code here.

01:15:17.270 --> 01:15:20.890 align:middle line:84%
And instead of just converting
the user's input x and y to

01:15:20.890 --> 01:15:23.950 align:middle line:84%
integers on line 1 and 2, let's
just make a simple change.

01:15:23.950 --> 01:15:27.400 align:middle line:84%
Let's actually convert it
to a float on the first line

01:15:27.400 --> 01:15:30.640 align:middle line:90%
and a float on the second line here.

01:15:30.640 --> 01:15:35.410 align:middle line:84%
Now I think, if I go down to my terminal
window and run Python of Calculator.py,

01:15:35.410 --> 01:15:39.040 align:middle line:84%
let's type in a number like
1.2 with a decimal point

01:15:39.040 --> 01:15:42.100 align:middle line:84%
and 3.4 with a decimal
point, and there we go.

01:15:42.100 --> 01:15:44.510 align:middle line:90%
We have 4.6 as the final answer.

01:15:44.510 --> 01:15:48.250 align:middle line:84%
So that wouldn't have worked before
if I was only expecting integers

01:15:48.250 --> 01:15:51.550 align:middle line:84%
from the user, but now that I'm
expecting floating point values

01:15:51.550 --> 01:15:56.180 align:middle line:84%
and accommodating it, I can actually now
do floating point arithmetic as well.

01:15:56.180 --> 01:15:59.020 align:middle line:84%
But suppose that I don't
really want the final answer

01:15:59.020 --> 01:16:03.160 align:middle line:90%
to be a floating point value like 4.6.

01:16:03.160 --> 01:16:05.930 align:middle line:84%
I would be happy if we just
round to the nearest integer.

01:16:05.930 --> 01:16:08.800 align:middle line:84%
So I want to support the
user typing in floating point

01:16:08.800 --> 01:16:11.120 align:middle line:84%
values with decimal points,
but at the end of the day,

01:16:11.120 --> 01:16:15.760 align:middle line:84%
I just want to round the result to the
nearest possible integer, for instance.

01:16:15.760 --> 01:16:20.830 align:middle line:84%
Well it turns out that here too Python
comes with some functionality built in.

01:16:20.830 --> 01:16:23.620 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, if we return
to this URL from earlier

01:16:23.620 --> 01:16:26.560 align:middle line:84%
wherein all of the Python
built-in functions are listed,

01:16:26.560 --> 01:16:29.200 align:middle line:84%
there's one called
"round" which does exactly

01:16:29.200 --> 01:16:31.510 align:middle line:84%
as we would expect, it
takes as input a number

01:16:31.510 --> 01:16:36.860 align:middle line:84%
and then rounds it for us, for
instance, to the nearest integer.

01:16:36.860 --> 01:16:40.390 align:middle line:84%
But if we look a little closer at
that documentation as we can here--

01:16:40.390 --> 01:16:41.710 align:middle line:90%
I'll provide an excerpt--

01:16:41.710 --> 01:16:45.400 align:middle line:84%
this is what the function looks
like in the documentation.

01:16:45.400 --> 01:16:48.550 align:middle line:84%
And recall that earlier we looked
at the documentation for print

01:16:48.550 --> 01:16:51.550 align:middle line:84%
and this is similar in spirit
that this shows us not just

01:16:51.550 --> 01:16:54.880 align:middle line:84%
the name of the function but its
available parameters, that is,

01:16:54.880 --> 01:16:57.850 align:middle line:84%
inputs that we can provide
when using this function.

01:16:57.850 --> 01:17:00.280 align:middle line:84%
But this is a little cryptic
too, just like Print was,

01:17:00.280 --> 01:17:01.640 align:middle line:90%
and it adds some syntax.

01:17:01.640 --> 01:17:02.290 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

01:17:02.290 --> 01:17:04.630 align:middle line:84%
The name of this function
here is of course Round

01:17:04.630 --> 01:17:07.450 align:middle line:90%
and its first argument is a number.

01:17:07.450 --> 01:17:10.548 align:middle line:84%
Notice this time there's no
star, there's no star objects

01:17:10.548 --> 01:17:11.590 align:middle line:90%
like there was for print.

01:17:11.590 --> 01:17:16.000 align:middle line:84%
The Round function takes just one
number as its first argument, period.

01:17:16.000 --> 01:17:18.820 align:middle line:90%
That's its positional parameter.

01:17:18.820 --> 01:17:20.420 align:middle line:90%
But notice this syntax.

01:17:20.420 --> 01:17:22.720 align:middle line:84%
And this is a convention in
programming or technology

01:17:22.720 --> 01:17:26.260 align:middle line:84%
more generally, generally speaking,
when you see square brackets

01:17:26.260 --> 01:17:28.630 align:middle line:84%
and documentation like
this, this means that you're

01:17:28.630 --> 01:17:30.370 align:middle line:90%
about to see something optional.

01:17:30.370 --> 01:17:32.350 align:middle line:84%
And so what this means
is that if you want

01:17:32.350 --> 01:17:37.480 align:middle line:84%
to specify more precisely the number of
digits that you want the round function

01:17:37.480 --> 01:17:43.760 align:middle line:84%
to round to, you can specify it here
by adding a comma and then that number.

01:17:43.760 --> 01:17:48.190 align:middle line:84%
So if we read the documentation, if
you don't specify a number of digits,

01:17:48.190 --> 01:17:52.000 align:middle line:84%
you just specify the number to round,
it rounds to the nearest integer.

01:17:52.000 --> 01:17:55.990 align:middle line:84%
But suppose you want around to the
tenths place, or the hundredths place

01:17:55.990 --> 01:17:58.330 align:middle line:84%
that is one or two digits
after the decimal point,

01:17:58.330 --> 01:18:04.167 align:middle line:84%
you could additionally pass in comma
1 or comma 2 to be more precise.

01:18:04.167 --> 01:18:06.250 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the
documentation, there is saying.

01:18:06.250 --> 01:18:10.460 align:middle line:84%
Let's see if we can't then translate
this to some actual code for us.

01:18:10.460 --> 01:18:13.630 align:middle line:84%
So if I go back now to
VS Code and I consider

01:18:13.630 --> 01:18:18.350 align:middle line:84%
that I want to go ahead and round x and
y, I can do this in a couple of ways.

01:18:18.350 --> 01:18:22.630 align:middle line:84%
I could do round x +
y, but you know, I'd

01:18:22.630 --> 01:18:25.210 align:middle line:84%
actually kind of prefer to break
this now out into two lines.

01:18:25.210 --> 01:18:28.300 align:middle line:84%
I don't have to, and reasonable
people here might disagree,

01:18:28.300 --> 01:18:31.330 align:middle line:84%
but I'd like to revert to a
scenario where I'm printing z,

01:18:31.330 --> 01:18:34.270 align:middle line:84%
so that I can just a little more
clearly to myself, to others,

01:18:34.270 --> 01:18:38.890 align:middle line:90%
say z = the rounded result of x + y.

01:18:38.890 --> 01:18:41.060 align:middle line:84%
It's not necessarily
the better way to do it,

01:18:41.060 --> 01:18:44.690 align:middle line:84%
but I'm a little more comfortable with
breaking out my thoughts one at a time,

01:18:44.690 --> 01:18:47.882 align:middle line:84%
especially if I want to start
commenting each of these chunks of code.

01:18:47.882 --> 01:18:49.840 align:middle line:84%
All right, let me go down
to my terminal window

01:18:49.840 --> 01:18:52.120 align:middle line:90%
now and run Python of Calculator.py.

01:18:52.120 --> 01:18:52.840 align:middle line:90%
What's x?

01:18:52.840 --> 01:18:54.490 align:middle line:90%
Let's do 1.2 again.

01:18:54.490 --> 01:18:56.260 align:middle line:90%
Then let's do 3.4.

01:18:56.260 --> 01:18:59.980 align:middle line:84%
And now it was previously
4.6, but now it's

01:18:59.980 --> 01:19:04.540 align:middle line:84%
been rounded up to the nearest integer,
which of course is going to be 5.

01:19:04.540 --> 01:19:09.050 align:middle line:84%
All right, what if I wanted to
change this a little further?

01:19:09.050 --> 01:19:11.830 align:middle line:84%
What if I wanted to support
maybe really big numbers,

01:19:11.830 --> 01:19:15.872 align:middle line:84%
big numbers, irrespective of rounding,
let's just do something like this.

01:19:15.872 --> 01:19:17.830 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and run
Python of Calculator.py

01:19:17.830 --> 01:19:22.270 align:middle line:90%
again and let me just add 999 + 1.

01:19:22.270 --> 01:19:25.030 align:middle line:84%
And notice, I don't have
to type decimal points,

01:19:25.030 --> 01:19:27.340 align:middle line:84%
even though I'm converting
to float, my program

01:19:27.340 --> 01:19:30.840 align:middle line:84%
will just allow me to type decimal
points, but I don't need to oblige.

01:19:30.840 --> 01:19:34.110 align:middle line:84%
The answer of course here should
be, and is in fact, 1,000,

01:19:34.110 --> 01:19:35.670 align:middle line:90%
whether or not we round.

01:19:35.670 --> 01:19:38.470 align:middle line:84%
So that's just arithmetic
with integers here.

01:19:38.470 --> 01:19:44.640 align:middle line:84%
But in the US, we tend to format
long numbers by putting commas

01:19:44.640 --> 01:19:47.580 align:middle line:90%
after or before every triple of digits.

01:19:47.580 --> 01:19:50.660 align:middle line:84%
Other countries flip it and they
use periods and commas instead.

01:19:50.660 --> 01:19:51.660 align:middle line:90%
That's a system setting.

01:19:51.660 --> 01:19:53.700 align:middle line:84%
You can change that
on your own Mac or PC

01:19:53.700 --> 01:19:57.000 align:middle line:84%
or device for Python or any
language, but for me, I'm

01:19:57.000 --> 01:20:01.200 align:middle line:84%
using the US approach here, which
is periods for decimal points

01:20:01.200 --> 01:20:02.940 align:middle line:90%
and commas for separators.

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:07.890 align:middle line:84%
What if I wanted this to
be outputted as 1,000?

01:20:07.890 --> 01:20:12.180 align:middle line:84%
Just to make it a little more clear that
it's 1,000 and not something like 100.

01:20:12.180 --> 01:20:14.580 align:middle line:84%
That's even more useful
when it's like one million--

01:20:14.580 --> 01:20:18.060 align:middle line:90%
1,000,000.

01:20:18.060 --> 01:20:19.950 align:middle line:84%
Wouldn't it be nice if
we could automatically

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:21.540 align:middle line:90%
output those numbers as well?

01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:23.700 align:middle line:90%
Well, it turns out that we can.

01:20:23.700 --> 01:20:28.410 align:middle line:84%
There is a way using Python
to actually specify that we

01:20:28.410 --> 01:20:30.540 align:middle line:90%
want to include commas like this.

01:20:30.540 --> 01:20:34.800 align:middle line:84%
And here we have an opportunity to
bring back our old friend, the F string.

01:20:34.800 --> 01:20:37.440 align:middle line:84%
First, let me do something
that's not that productive.

01:20:37.440 --> 01:20:38.760 align:middle line:90%
First let me do this.

01:20:38.760 --> 01:20:41.610 align:middle line:84%
Let me print out the value
of z, but wait a minute.

01:20:41.610 --> 01:20:44.850 align:middle line:84%
I can't just say "z" because
that's literally going to print z

01:20:44.850 --> 01:20:45.820 align:middle line:90%
on the screen.

01:20:45.820 --> 01:20:49.050 align:middle line:84%
So let me wrap it with those
curly braces like I did before,

01:20:49.050 --> 01:20:50.550 align:middle line:90%
but that too was not enough.

01:20:50.550 --> 01:20:54.060 align:middle line:84%
I literally needed to add an F
at the beginning of my string

01:20:54.060 --> 01:20:56.940 align:middle line:84%
to tell Python that this is
an F string, a format string.

01:20:56.940 --> 01:21:01.650 align:middle line:84%
That now is going to print out, not
very interestingly, just the value of z

01:21:01.650 --> 01:21:02.320 align:middle line:90%
itself.

01:21:02.320 --> 01:21:06.270 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to great lengths just to
print z when really I could have just

01:21:06.270 --> 01:21:07.890 align:middle line:90%
passed z as the sole argument.

01:21:07.890 --> 01:21:11.820 align:middle line:84%
But just to ensure that I haven't
broken it, let's do this again.

01:21:11.820 --> 01:21:14.190 align:middle line:90%
999 + 1, Enter.

01:21:14.190 --> 01:21:15.300 align:middle line:90%
OK, it's still 1,000.

01:21:15.300 --> 01:21:16.950 align:middle line:90%
So I didn't make anything worse.

01:21:16.950 --> 01:21:20.460 align:middle line:84%
But notice-- and this syntax is
unfortunately a bit cryptic--

01:21:20.460 --> 01:21:22.380 align:middle line:90%
notice that I can actually do this.

01:21:22.380 --> 01:21:27.450 align:middle line:84%
I can put a colon after the z
and I can put a comma thereafter.

01:21:27.450 --> 01:21:29.763 align:middle line:84%
This looks very cryptic,
admittedly, and even I

01:21:29.763 --> 01:21:32.430 align:middle line:84%
have to constantly look things
like this up in the documentation

01:21:32.430 --> 01:21:33.750 align:middle line:90%
to remember the syntax.

01:21:33.750 --> 01:21:35.460 align:middle line:90%
But here, let me run it again.

01:21:35.460 --> 01:21:41.250 align:middle line:84%
Python of Calculator.py,
999 1 and now notice

01:21:41.250 --> 01:21:43.928 align:middle line:84%
that the number has been
automatically formatted for me.

01:21:43.928 --> 01:21:45.720 align:middle line:84%
If I were in a different
country or locale,

01:21:45.720 --> 01:21:49.620 align:middle line:84%
I could absolutely override this to
use periods instead of commas or vise

01:21:49.620 --> 01:21:50.290 align:middle line:90%
versa.

01:21:50.290 --> 01:21:52.990 align:middle line:84%
But in this case here, it's just
happening for me automatically.

01:21:52.990 --> 01:21:56.310 align:middle line:84%
So there too we see a hint of what
it means to really format a string.

01:21:56.310 --> 01:21:57.720 align:middle line:90%
There's even more power--

01:21:57.720 --> 01:22:00.633 align:middle line:84%
more powerful capabilities
built into that.

01:22:00.633 --> 01:22:02.550 align:middle line:84%
All right, let me pause
here to see if there's

01:22:02.550 --> 01:22:10.200 align:middle line:84%
any questions now on floats, on
rounding, or on this use of F strings.

01:22:10.200 --> 01:22:11.700 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes, so I have a question.

01:22:11.700 --> 01:22:14.760 align:middle line:84%
So when using floats,
is there like a cap

01:22:14.760 --> 01:22:16.860 align:middle line:90%
to how many decimal points it can have?

01:22:16.860 --> 01:22:18.360 align:middle line:90%
DAVID MALAN: A really good question.

01:22:18.360 --> 01:22:21.150 align:middle line:84%
So floats, yes, and this is a
problem we'll revisit before long.

01:22:21.150 --> 01:22:25.590 align:middle line:84%
Floats cannot represent
numbers infinitely precisely.

01:22:25.590 --> 01:22:28.112 align:middle line:84%
In a nutshell, because computers
only have so much memory.

01:22:28.112 --> 01:22:29.820 align:middle line:84%
They only have a finite
amount of memory.

01:22:29.820 --> 01:22:34.200 align:middle line:84%
You and I only have a finite amount
of hardware inside of the computer,

01:22:34.200 --> 01:22:36.540 align:middle line:84%
so at some point, they're
going to have to round.

01:22:36.540 --> 01:22:38.190 align:middle line:90%
Right now I'm rounding automatically.

01:22:38.190 --> 01:22:40.732 align:middle line:84%
Effectively computers will
eventually have to do that for us,

01:22:40.732 --> 01:22:44.010 align:middle line:84%
but we'll see that as a
fundamental problem before long.

01:22:44.010 --> 01:22:47.385 align:middle line:84%
Allow me to turn back just for
a few final examples on float

01:22:47.385 --> 01:22:50.010 align:middle line:84%
before we introduce a few final
examples that allow us not just

01:22:50.010 --> 01:22:52.290 align:middle line:90%
to use functions, but to make our own.

01:22:52.290 --> 01:22:56.940 align:middle line:84%
Let me propose that we also try our
hands at a bit of division here.

01:22:56.940 --> 01:22:59.250 align:middle line:84%
Let me propose that we
modify this calculator now

01:22:59.250 --> 01:23:01.740 align:middle line:84%
to still take a couple of
floats, but let's now just do

01:23:01.740 --> 01:23:04.050 align:middle line:90%
something a little simpler than--

01:23:04.050 --> 01:23:07.085 align:middle line:84%
a little different from this,
just doing x divided by y.

01:23:07.085 --> 01:23:09.210 align:middle line:84%
And let me go ahead and
get rid of my format string

01:23:09.210 --> 01:23:12.383 align:middle line:84%
and just keep it simple for
now, printing out z instead.

01:23:12.383 --> 01:23:13.800 align:middle line:90%
And what are we going to see here?

01:23:13.800 --> 01:23:15.092 align:middle line:90%
Well just some simple division.

01:23:15.092 --> 01:23:19.920 align:middle line:84%
So Python of Calculator.py, let's
do something like 2 divided by 3,

01:23:19.920 --> 01:23:21.960 align:middle line:90%
and of course I get 0.66666.

01:23:21.960 --> 01:23:25.600 align:middle line:84%
And to Ethan's question a moment
ago, it does seem to be finite.

01:23:25.600 --> 01:23:30.180 align:middle line:84%
It's not rounding in a weird way here,
but I only seem to see so many digits.

01:23:30.180 --> 01:23:34.170 align:middle line:84%
That's an inevitability of
using a float in this way.

01:23:34.170 --> 01:23:37.620 align:middle line:84%
By contrast, just so you know,
integers nowadays in Python

01:23:37.620 --> 01:23:39.690 align:middle line:90%
can be as big as you want them to be.

01:23:39.690 --> 01:23:41.910 align:middle line:84%
Unlike other languages,
there is no upper bound

01:23:41.910 --> 01:23:44.550 align:middle line:84%
on how big an INT can be
now in Python, but there

01:23:44.550 --> 01:23:48.225 align:middle line:84%
is a bound on just how precise
a floating point value can be.

01:23:48.225 --> 01:23:50.850 align:middle line:84%
All right, now that I've got some
simple division working here,

01:23:50.850 --> 01:23:52.380 align:middle line:90%
let's go ahead and round this.

01:23:52.380 --> 01:23:57.060 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice to round this really
long number 0.6666666 and so forth

01:23:57.060 --> 01:23:59.190 align:middle line:90%
to maybe just two decimal places.

01:23:59.190 --> 01:24:02.460 align:middle line:84%
We've seen how to do this with round,
though, at least in its documentation.

01:24:02.460 --> 01:24:04.980 align:middle line:84%
Let's just round this
not to the nearest INT,

01:24:04.980 --> 01:24:09.810 align:middle line:84%
by passing in just x divided by y,
which is one argument, once the math is

01:24:09.810 --> 01:24:13.350 align:middle line:84%
done inside of the parentheses, I don't
want to pass in just one argument.

01:24:13.350 --> 01:24:17.100 align:middle line:84%
I want to pass in two so
that I can specify n digits,

01:24:17.100 --> 01:24:21.030 align:middle line:84%
number of digits, which you'll recall
was the second parameter for round.

01:24:21.030 --> 01:24:23.240 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and run
Python of Calculator.py.

01:24:23.240 --> 01:24:24.240 align:middle line:90%
I'll do the same thing--

01:24:24.240 --> 01:24:27.300 align:middle line:90%
2 and then 3, 0.67.

01:24:27.300 --> 01:24:31.130 align:middle line:84%
So here too we see a way of rounding
now, not just to a nearest integer,

01:24:31.130 --> 01:24:34.160 align:middle line:90%
but to a nearest number of digits.

01:24:34.160 --> 01:24:36.300 align:middle line:90%
But there's another way to do this here.

01:24:36.300 --> 01:24:40.920 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, this evokes
our F string example again.

01:24:40.920 --> 01:24:42.270 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead and change this.

01:24:42.270 --> 01:24:45.740 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that you didn't remember the
round function or, for some reason,

01:24:45.740 --> 01:24:46.940 align:middle line:90%
you didn't want to use it.

01:24:46.940 --> 01:24:49.340 align:middle line:84%
You instead want to just
use a format string.

01:24:49.340 --> 01:24:50.510 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's go there.

01:24:50.510 --> 01:24:55.280 align:middle line:84%
Let me do "z" but let me surround
it with those curly braces.

01:24:55.280 --> 01:24:58.850 align:middle line:84%
Let me add the F at the beginning, and
again, this is not interesting yet.

01:24:58.850 --> 01:25:02.300 align:middle line:84%
This is just going to print out z,
but I'm adding a lot more complexity

01:25:02.300 --> 01:25:03.920 align:middle line:90%
to turn it into an F string.

01:25:03.920 --> 01:25:08.940 align:middle line:84%
But notice I can do something else
after my variable name, after the colon.

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:11.300 align:middle line:84%
If this were going to
be a big integer, I

01:25:11.300 --> 01:25:15.200 align:middle line:84%
might want to use a comma like before
to separate each triple of numbers

01:25:15.200 --> 01:25:15.950 align:middle line:90%
with commas.

01:25:15.950 --> 01:25:16.580 align:middle line:90%
But I don't.

01:25:16.580 --> 01:25:20.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use a different
sequence of characters.

01:25:20.120 --> 01:25:25.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say 0.2F and this too is
one of these very cryptic things I have

01:25:25.190 --> 01:25:28.040 align:middle line:84%
to constantly look up because I
forget if I don't use it that often.

01:25:28.040 --> 01:25:32.030 align:middle line:84%
So don't be intimidated if this
looks especially weird, but this is,

01:25:32.030 --> 01:25:33.950 align:middle line:84%
according to the
documentation, the way you

01:25:33.950 --> 01:25:37.830 align:middle line:84%
specify using an F string how
many digits you want to print.

01:25:37.830 --> 01:25:39.980 align:middle line:84%
So let me run this
version of the calculator.

01:25:39.980 --> 01:25:43.170 align:middle line:84%
Type in 2 and then 3, we
get the exact same thing.

01:25:43.170 --> 01:25:45.350 align:middle line:84%
But again, this is just
consistent with my claim

01:25:45.350 --> 01:25:49.580 align:middle line:84%
that in programming we can so
very often solve the same problem

01:25:49.580 --> 01:25:51.030 align:middle line:90%
in multiple ways.

01:25:51.030 --> 01:25:56.240 align:middle line:84%
This is just now the F string
approach to that very same problem.

01:25:56.240 --> 01:25:57.630 align:middle line:90%
All right, which one is better?

01:25:57.630 --> 01:25:58.310 align:middle line:90%
It depends.

01:25:58.310 --> 01:26:00.200 align:middle line:90%
In this case, they're pretty equivalent.

01:26:00.200 --> 01:26:03.260 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine, though, it being
useful to use a function sometimes

01:26:03.260 --> 01:26:07.340 align:middle line:84%
so that you can pass in an argument
like n digits as that second argument,

01:26:07.340 --> 01:26:10.700 align:middle line:84%
or you can imagine just deciding
in advance that you want 0.2

01:26:10.700 --> 01:26:13.370 align:middle line:90%
and then writing it like this.

01:26:13.370 --> 01:26:17.300 align:middle line:84%
Let's transition now from focusing
on strings and on integers and on

01:26:17.300 --> 01:26:19.880 align:middle line:84%
floats to focusing now
on functions themselves.

01:26:19.880 --> 01:26:21.860 align:middle line:84%
We began today by
focusing on how you can

01:26:21.860 --> 01:26:24.230 align:middle line:90%
use functions that come with Python.

01:26:24.230 --> 01:26:26.990 align:middle line:84%
But wouldn't it be nice if you
could invent your own functions,

01:26:26.990 --> 01:26:29.330 align:middle line:84%
especially if, to our
point earlier, you find

01:26:29.330 --> 01:26:32.210 align:middle line:84%
yourself solving the same kind
of problem again and again?

01:26:32.210 --> 01:26:34.700 align:middle line:84%
It's nice that Python comes
with the print function

01:26:34.700 --> 01:26:37.130 align:middle line:84%
because it's really useful to be
able to print things on the screen,

01:26:37.130 --> 01:26:40.130 align:middle line:84%
but wouldn't it be nice if you could
print specific things on the screen

01:26:40.130 --> 01:26:42.170 align:middle line:90%
by just calling your own function?

01:26:42.170 --> 01:26:44.100 align:middle line:90%
Well let me propose that we do this.

01:26:44.100 --> 01:26:49.760 align:middle line:84%
Let me go back to VS Code here and let
me propose that we go back to Hello.py.

01:26:49.760 --> 01:26:52.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to reopen Hello.py
where we left it before

01:26:52.820 --> 01:26:54.620 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to go
ahead now and propose

01:26:54.620 --> 01:26:58.460 align:middle line:84%
that we consider how we can start
improving this further by making

01:26:58.460 --> 01:26:59.330 align:middle line:90%
our own function.

01:26:59.330 --> 01:27:03.380 align:middle line:84%
I have written so many programs
today that just say Hello

01:27:03.380 --> 01:27:05.360 align:middle line:90%
and each time I'm using print.

01:27:05.360 --> 01:27:08.090 align:middle line:84%
But wouldn't it have been nice
if, from the beginning of today,

01:27:08.090 --> 01:27:12.620 align:middle line:84%
we could just call a function called
Hello that just says Hello for us?

01:27:12.620 --> 01:27:15.080 align:middle line:84%
Now the authors of
Python years ago didn't

01:27:15.080 --> 01:27:18.020 align:middle line:84%
think that we need a special
function just to say Hello,

01:27:18.020 --> 01:27:19.820 align:middle line:90%
but I would like that to exist.

01:27:19.820 --> 01:27:21.980 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying Hello so
many times, I just want

01:27:21.980 --> 01:27:23.600 align:middle line:90%
to be able to call a function Hello.

01:27:23.600 --> 01:27:25.550 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to start from scratch here.

01:27:25.550 --> 01:27:27.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to delete all
of my code from earlier

01:27:27.680 --> 01:27:32.510 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to pretend for the moment
that a function called Hello exists.

01:27:32.510 --> 01:27:34.250 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to do
just as I did before.

01:27:34.250 --> 01:27:36.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get the user's
name with the input function,

01:27:36.740 --> 01:27:39.110 align:middle line:90%
asking what's your name, question mark.

01:27:39.110 --> 01:27:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to
call a function Hello

01:27:42.230 --> 01:27:45.260 align:middle line:84%
and then I'm going to
print out the user's name.

01:27:45.260 --> 01:27:50.190 align:middle line:84%
Now I will admit, Hello doesn't exist,
so bad things are about to happen,

01:27:50.190 --> 01:27:51.440 align:middle line:90%
but let's see what.

01:27:51.440 --> 01:27:53.210 align:middle line:90%
Let me go down to my terminal window.

01:27:53.210 --> 01:27:55.700 align:middle line:90%
Let me run Python of Hello.py.

01:27:55.700 --> 01:27:59.030 align:middle line:84%
I think the first line is going to
be OK because that worked before.

01:27:59.030 --> 01:28:01.280 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, it's
prompting me for my name.

01:28:01.280 --> 01:28:02.780 align:middle line:90%
So let me type in David.

01:28:02.780 --> 01:28:05.090 align:middle line:84%
The second line of code
is apparently calling

01:28:05.090 --> 01:28:08.840 align:middle line:84%
a function that looks like it's called
Hello, because why is it a function?

01:28:08.840 --> 01:28:11.820 align:middle line:84%
It has a parenthesis and a closed
parenthesis immediately after it.

01:28:11.820 --> 01:28:14.300 align:middle line:84%
And that's what every function
we've used has looked like.

01:28:14.300 --> 01:28:16.340 align:middle line:84%
But Python is not going
to recognize this one.

01:28:16.340 --> 01:28:18.890 align:middle line:84%
When I hit Enter now,
I get a name error.

01:28:18.890 --> 01:28:21.950 align:middle line:84%
Name "Hello" is not
defined, did you mean Help?

01:28:21.950 --> 01:28:26.120 align:middle line:84%
I didn't, although it's opportune that's
what I need at this point is some help,

01:28:26.120 --> 01:28:29.540 align:middle line:84%
but I am encountering
this error because why?

01:28:29.540 --> 01:28:31.440 align:middle line:90%
The function just doesn't exist.

01:28:31.440 --> 01:28:33.350 align:middle line:90%
So how do I make this function exist?

01:28:33.350 --> 01:28:39.440 align:middle line:84%
Well I need to create it myself using
this key word, DEF, DEF for define.

01:28:39.440 --> 01:28:43.610 align:middle line:84%
So here too, just as STR is short for
string and INT is short for integer,

01:28:43.610 --> 01:28:45.830 align:middle line:90%
DEF is short for define.

01:28:45.830 --> 01:28:50.330 align:middle line:84%
If and when you want to define,
create, invent your own functions,

01:28:50.330 --> 01:28:53.870 align:middle line:84%
you can do so using now
this keyword in Python.

01:28:53.870 --> 01:28:58.310 align:middle line:84%
So let me go back to my code here and
let me propose that we define this

01:28:58.310 --> 01:28:59.900 align:middle line:90%
perhaps in this way.

01:28:59.900 --> 01:29:02.660 align:middle line:84%
At the very top of my file,
I'm going to first take

01:29:02.660 --> 01:29:05.150 align:middle line:84%
a moment to define a
function called Hello

01:29:05.150 --> 01:29:10.430 align:middle line:84%
using DEF Hello, open parenthesis,
close parenthesis, colon.

01:29:10.430 --> 01:29:15.110 align:middle line:84%
What this means now is that Python
is going to treat every line of code

01:29:15.110 --> 01:29:20.240 align:middle line:84%
that I indent underneath this one as
the meaning of this new function, Hello.

01:29:20.240 --> 01:29:23.030 align:middle line:90%
So DEF is important as is the space.

01:29:23.030 --> 01:29:26.577 align:middle line:84%
I get to choose the name of the function
and I'm choosing to call it Hello.

01:29:26.577 --> 01:29:29.660 align:middle line:84%
The parentheses with nothing inside
means that this function at the moment

01:29:29.660 --> 01:29:32.540 align:middle line:84%
is not going to take any
inputs, no arguments there too.

01:29:32.540 --> 01:29:35.810 align:middle line:84%
The colon means, stay
tuned for some indentation.

01:29:35.810 --> 01:29:38.750 align:middle line:84%
Everything that's indented
beneath this line of code

01:29:38.750 --> 01:29:40.310 align:middle line:90%
is going to be part of this function.

01:29:40.310 --> 01:29:42.920 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be a super short
function-- one line of code--

01:29:42.920 --> 01:29:46.040 align:middle line:90%
it's just going to print out "Hello."

01:29:46.040 --> 01:29:51.830 align:middle line:84%
But now on lines 1 and 2, I have
invented my own function Hello.

01:29:51.830 --> 01:29:54.740 align:middle line:84%
Notice these dots that have
now magically appeared here.

01:29:54.740 --> 01:29:56.660 align:middle line:84%
This is just a setting
of my text editor,

01:29:56.660 --> 01:30:00.470 align:middle line:84%
VS Code in this case, that's just
making super explicit to me that I've

01:30:00.470 --> 01:30:04.160 align:middle line:84%
hit the space bar four times, or
equivalently the Tab key once,

01:30:04.160 --> 01:30:07.018 align:middle line:84%
which is converted
automatically to four spaces.

01:30:07.018 --> 01:30:10.310 align:middle line:84%
Generally speaking, I'm going to need to
make sure that all of my indented code

01:30:10.310 --> 01:30:14.150 align:middle line:84%
lines up now so that Python knows
that it's all part of the same thing.

01:30:14.150 --> 01:30:16.770 align:middle line:84%
But it's easy in this case
because it's just a single line.

01:30:16.770 --> 01:30:20.810 align:middle line:84%
But now, thanks to lines 1
and 2, the function Hello

01:30:20.810 --> 01:30:24.920 align:middle line:84%
will absolutely exist when
I'm ready to use it on line 6.

01:30:24.920 --> 01:30:29.180 align:middle line:84%
So let me go down to my terminal window
and run Python of Hello.py, Enter.

01:30:29.180 --> 01:30:30.570 align:middle line:90%
Here comes my name again.

01:30:30.570 --> 01:30:35.120 align:middle line:84%
And now when I hit Enter,
I now see Hello, David.

01:30:35.120 --> 01:30:37.190 align:middle line:84%
All right, we've kind of
regressed though, right?

01:30:37.190 --> 01:30:39.960 align:middle line:84%
This is not nearly as
pretty as it once was.

01:30:39.960 --> 01:30:44.840 align:middle line:84%
I think we can probably do better
than this by improving things further.

01:30:44.840 --> 01:30:47.510 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we consider,
though, how we might

01:30:47.510 --> 01:30:50.480 align:middle line:90%
say parameterize this same function?

01:30:50.480 --> 01:30:55.010 align:middle line:84%
That is to say, can we customize Hello
to maybe take the user's name as input

01:30:55.010 --> 01:31:00.020 align:middle line:84%
so that we can say, not only Hello, but
the person's name all on one line, all

01:31:00.020 --> 01:31:01.070 align:middle line:90%
in one breath?

01:31:01.070 --> 01:31:02.450 align:middle line:90%
Well I think we can do this.

01:31:02.450 --> 01:31:05.400 align:middle line:84%
Let me propose that
we do this as follows.

01:31:05.400 --> 01:31:10.100 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and up in my code,
let me inside of these parentheses,

01:31:10.100 --> 01:31:12.350 align:middle line:84%
let me come up with
my own parameter name.

01:31:12.350 --> 01:31:14.510 align:middle line:84%
I have complete choice
here and I'm going

01:31:14.510 --> 01:31:17.780 align:middle line:84%
to say that the name of my
parameter will be the word To.

01:31:17.780 --> 01:31:18.500 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:31:18.500 --> 01:31:22.970 align:middle line:84%
Because I want my function to
sound like the verb it represents--

01:31:22.970 --> 01:31:23.690 align:middle line:90%
Hello.

01:31:23.690 --> 01:31:25.610 align:middle line:90%
But who do you want to say Hello to?

01:31:25.610 --> 01:31:27.860 align:middle line:84%
Well I'm going to call my
parameter for this function

01:31:27.860 --> 01:31:30.860 align:middle line:84%
To, just because in English,
it kind of sounds nice to me.

01:31:30.860 --> 01:31:32.900 align:middle line:84%
Hello To, who do you
want to say Hello to?

01:31:32.900 --> 01:31:36.050 align:middle line:84%
That's why I'm calling this
parameter To instead of something

01:31:36.050 --> 01:31:38.360 align:middle line:90%
simpler like x or y or z.

01:31:38.360 --> 01:31:40.798 align:middle line:84%
All right, well what do I
want to do with the word To?

01:31:40.798 --> 01:31:42.590 align:middle line:84%
Well I can do a couple
of different things.

01:31:42.590 --> 01:31:45.320 align:middle line:84%
We've seen like so many different
ways to implement Hello.

01:31:45.320 --> 01:31:48.000 align:middle line:84%
Let me just add a comma
there for grammar's sake.

01:31:48.000 --> 01:31:52.700 align:middle line:84%
And then let me put the word To
after that as the second argument

01:31:52.700 --> 01:31:53.715 align:middle line:90%
to the function Hello.

01:31:53.715 --> 01:31:56.090 align:middle line:84%
There's other ways we can do
this and we've seen so many,

01:31:56.090 --> 01:31:58.940 align:middle line:84%
but this one looks a little
clear to me, I'll say.

01:31:58.940 --> 01:32:00.380 align:middle line:90%
What's going to happen next?

01:32:00.380 --> 01:32:03.470 align:middle line:84%
Well I don't think I need
this extra print line here.

01:32:03.470 --> 01:32:07.040 align:middle line:84%
I think what I'm going to do is
this, I'm going to go ahead here

01:32:07.040 --> 01:32:12.740 align:middle line:84%
and print out not the person's name
manually, I'm going to instead say,

01:32:12.740 --> 01:32:15.410 align:middle line:90%
Hello parentheses name.

01:32:15.410 --> 01:32:16.880 align:middle line:90%
So what am I now doing?

01:32:16.880 --> 01:32:20.690 align:middle line:84%
On lines 1 and 2 I'm defining my
very own function called Hello,

01:32:20.690 --> 01:32:23.240 align:middle line:84%
but this time that
function has been designed

01:32:23.240 --> 01:32:26.750 align:middle line:84%
to take a parameter, a
single parameter, as input.

01:32:26.750 --> 01:32:29.810 align:middle line:84%
And I'm using the value
of that parameter which

01:32:29.810 --> 01:32:33.470 align:middle line:84%
I called To to plug into
print so that I see not only

01:32:33.470 --> 01:32:35.780 align:middle line:90%
Hello but also that person's name.

01:32:35.780 --> 01:32:37.070 align:middle line:90%
What am I doing on line 5?

01:32:37.070 --> 01:32:39.140 align:middle line:84%
Same as always, I'm just
getting the user's name.

01:32:39.140 --> 01:32:42.140 align:middle line:84%
Line 6, I'm not only
calling Hello, I'm passing

01:32:42.140 --> 01:32:46.500 align:middle line:84%
as input the name
variable as an argument

01:32:46.500 --> 01:32:48.912 align:middle line:84%
so that that's what
gets passed into Hello.

01:32:48.912 --> 01:32:50.870 align:middle line:84%
And what's happening here
is essentially this--

01:32:50.870 --> 01:32:54.710 align:middle line:84%
even though the variable is called
Name here, when the function

01:32:54.710 --> 01:33:00.920 align:middle line:84%
itself is called, the computer assumes
that same value is now called To.

01:33:00.920 --> 01:33:04.760 align:middle line:84%
So Name is essentially copied
to another variable called

01:33:04.760 --> 01:33:11.420 align:middle line:84%
To so that in the context of Hello, I
can say Hello to that variable instead.

01:33:11.420 --> 01:33:15.140 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see in a moment what happens
if we don't keep those straight.

01:33:15.140 --> 01:33:17.600 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and run
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

01:33:17.600 --> 01:33:18.540 align:middle line:90%
What's your name?

01:33:18.540 --> 01:33:20.060 align:middle line:90%
And now I'm crossing my fingers.

01:33:20.060 --> 01:33:20.840 align:middle line:90%
Enter.

01:33:20.840 --> 01:33:21.750 align:middle line:90%
There we go.

01:33:21.750 --> 01:33:25.700 align:middle line:84%
We're back in business, but now I have
my own custom function called Hello

01:33:25.700 --> 01:33:28.550 align:middle line:84%
that's allowing me to say
Hello to a specific person.

01:33:28.550 --> 01:33:30.980 align:middle line:84%
And here's where now things
can get really fancy.

01:33:30.980 --> 01:33:34.220 align:middle line:84%
What if you wanted your Hello function
to say Hello to someone specific,

01:33:34.220 --> 01:33:37.070 align:middle line:84%
but, you know what, if you don't
know who you want to say Hello to,

01:33:37.070 --> 01:33:38.840 align:middle line:84%
you want to say Hello
to the whole world,

01:33:38.840 --> 01:33:41.300 align:middle line:90%
you can give parameters default values.

01:33:41.300 --> 01:33:42.060 align:middle line:90%
We've seen that.

01:33:42.060 --> 01:33:45.530 align:middle line:84%
Recall that with print there
was a default value for SEP,

01:33:45.530 --> 01:33:46.550 align:middle line:90%
for the separator.

01:33:46.550 --> 01:33:49.700 align:middle line:84%
There was a default value
for END, the line ending.

01:33:49.700 --> 01:33:52.280 align:middle line:84%
We can do that too,
and here's the syntax.

01:33:52.280 --> 01:33:56.390 align:middle line:84%
If you want the value of
this parameter by default,

01:33:56.390 --> 01:34:00.620 align:middle line:84%
if not provided by the programmer,
to be equal to "world,"

01:34:00.620 --> 01:34:05.155 align:middle line:84%
you literally do that in the same
line you're defining the function.

01:34:05.155 --> 01:34:07.280 align:middle line:84%
And I'll admit, it's starting
to look more cryptic,

01:34:07.280 --> 01:34:10.580 align:middle line:84%
but I'm still just defining
a function called Hello, it

01:34:10.580 --> 01:34:13.820 align:middle line:84%
takes a parameter called
To, but I'm assigning it

01:34:13.820 --> 01:34:19.340 align:middle line:84%
with the equal sign a default value of
"world," just in case the programmer

01:34:19.340 --> 01:34:22.310 align:middle line:90%
doesn't call Hello with an argument.

01:34:22.310 --> 01:34:23.660 align:middle line:90%
And we can see this here.

01:34:23.660 --> 01:34:26.750 align:middle line:84%
Let me change my code to
use Hello in two ways.

01:34:26.750 --> 01:34:31.880 align:middle line:84%
On line 5, I'm going to very
simply call Hello, no arguments.

01:34:31.880 --> 01:34:33.710 align:middle line:84%
Then on line 6, I'm
going to get the name.

01:34:33.710 --> 01:34:36.320 align:middle line:84%
Line 7, I'm going to call
Hello with an argument.

01:34:36.320 --> 01:34:39.060 align:middle line:84%
So you'll see Hello now
being used in two ways.

01:34:39.060 --> 01:34:41.960 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and
run Python of Hello.py.

01:34:41.960 --> 01:34:43.310 align:middle line:90%
I'll type in my name.

01:34:43.310 --> 01:34:44.630 align:middle line:90%
Oh, interesting.

01:34:44.630 --> 01:34:47.120 align:middle line:84%
Notice I already see
Hello, world, but that's

01:34:47.120 --> 01:34:52.070 align:middle line:84%
expected because line 5 happens before
line 6, but once I type my name,

01:34:52.070 --> 01:34:55.940 align:middle line:84%
now the program is going to be a
little more polite and say hello to me

01:34:55.940 --> 01:34:57.180 align:middle line:90%
personally.

01:34:57.180 --> 01:35:01.550 align:middle line:84%
So there too, we see with
relatively simple but new syntax

01:35:01.550 --> 01:35:05.990 align:middle line:84%
how you can implement functionality
very similar in spirit to what the print

01:35:05.990 --> 01:35:07.860 align:middle line:90%
function gave us automatically.

01:35:07.860 --> 01:35:10.940 align:middle line:84%
Now you have control
over doing that yourself.

01:35:10.940 --> 01:35:12.980 align:middle line:90%
But let me now make this point too.

01:35:12.980 --> 01:35:15.530 align:middle line:84%
One of the whole points of
defining your own functions

01:35:15.530 --> 01:35:18.680 align:middle line:84%
is one, just to avoid having to
repeat yourself again and again.

01:35:18.680 --> 01:35:21.890 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to actually
keep reinventing the wheel

01:35:21.890 --> 01:35:24.480 align:middle line:84%
and keep using the print function
again, and again, and again,

01:35:24.480 --> 01:35:25.850 align:middle line:90%
if you just want to say Hello.

01:35:25.850 --> 01:35:29.780 align:middle line:84%
Wouldn't it be nice now
if I could move this code

01:35:29.780 --> 01:35:34.010 align:middle line:84%
that I wrote for defining the Hello
function, and just to be dramatic,

01:35:34.010 --> 01:35:37.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to hit Enter a whole
lot of times, 50 lines down,

01:35:37.760 --> 01:35:41.240 align:middle line:84%
and put my definition of Hello
way further down in this file.

01:35:41.240 --> 01:35:41.840 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:35:41.840 --> 01:35:44.720 align:middle line:84%
Well, just for in the spirit
of out of sight, out of mind,

01:35:44.720 --> 01:35:48.800 align:middle line:84%
because if I now rewind to
the start of my program, now

01:35:48.800 --> 01:35:51.240 align:middle line:84%
you can take for granted
that, oh, Hello is a function.

01:35:51.240 --> 01:35:51.740 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:35:51.740 --> 01:35:54.740 align:middle line:84%
Because it's there on line 1 and it
has an open parenthesis and a closed

01:35:54.740 --> 01:35:57.560 align:middle line:84%
parenthesis, which, up until now
has meant, call this function.

01:35:57.560 --> 01:36:00.200 align:middle line:84%
And then on line 2 we're
getting a variable from the user

01:36:00.200 --> 01:36:04.620 align:middle line:84%
by typing in their name and then we're
calling Hello, passing in that value.

01:36:04.620 --> 01:36:07.790 align:middle line:84%
Well at this point, I can just
take for granted that Hello exists,

01:36:07.790 --> 01:36:11.360 align:middle line:84%
even if it's way down further in the
file or, as we'll see in future weeks,

01:36:11.360 --> 01:36:13.970 align:middle line:84%
even if it's in a
different file altogether.

01:36:13.970 --> 01:36:15.500 align:middle line:90%
But there's a problem here.

01:36:15.500 --> 01:36:19.190 align:middle line:84%
And let me go ahead and run
this version of Hello.py.

01:36:19.190 --> 01:36:23.270 align:middle line:84%
Notice that as soon as I run the
interpreter, Python of Hello.py,

01:36:23.270 --> 01:36:26.360 align:middle line:84%
I see a name error, name
Hello is not defined.

01:36:26.360 --> 01:36:28.280 align:middle line:90%
Again, did you mean Help?

01:36:28.280 --> 01:36:29.480 align:middle line:90%
Well, again, fitting.

01:36:29.480 --> 01:36:33.500 align:middle line:84%
I do need some help here, but I
didn't mean to call the function Help.

01:36:33.500 --> 01:36:36.650 align:middle line:84%
The problem here, though, is that
Python is just taking me literally.

01:36:36.650 --> 01:36:40.610 align:middle line:84%
I have defined my function
Hello all the way down here,

01:36:40.610 --> 01:36:42.500 align:middle line:90%
but I'm trying to use it way up here.

01:36:42.500 --> 01:36:43.465 align:middle line:90%
And that's not allowed.

01:36:43.465 --> 01:36:45.590 align:middle line:84%
Python's interpreter is
going to take you literally

01:36:45.590 --> 01:36:47.810 align:middle line:84%
and if you use a
function, it must already

01:36:47.810 --> 01:36:50.390 align:middle line:90%
exist by the time you are calling it.

01:36:50.390 --> 01:36:51.600 align:middle line:90%
So how do I fix this?

01:36:51.600 --> 01:36:53.270 align:middle line:90%
Well, apparently I can't do that.

01:36:53.270 --> 01:36:57.440 align:middle line:84%
I have to define any functions I
want at the very top of my file,

01:36:57.440 --> 01:36:59.720 align:middle line:84%
but that too could get
me into a bit of trouble

01:36:59.720 --> 01:37:04.640 align:middle line:84%
eventually because if I constantly have
to define a function above where I want

01:37:04.640 --> 01:37:07.010 align:middle line:84%
to use it, you're kind of
writing code in reverse,

01:37:07.010 --> 01:37:09.218 align:middle line:84%
you're constantly writing
functions up here, up here,

01:37:09.218 --> 01:37:12.450 align:middle line:84%
up here, as opposed to writing
your code logically, top to bottom.

01:37:12.450 --> 01:37:16.190 align:middle line:84%
So let me fix this in a more
standard way, which is to do this.

01:37:16.190 --> 01:37:20.990 align:middle line:84%
Generally speaking, you do want
to put the main part of your code

01:37:20.990 --> 01:37:22.530 align:middle line:90%
at the top of your file.

01:37:22.530 --> 01:37:27.110 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, I'm going to go so far
as to define my function, called Main.

01:37:27.110 --> 01:37:29.540 align:middle line:84%
It's not a requirement, but
it's in data convention,

01:37:29.540 --> 01:37:31.910 align:middle line:84%
and this just connotes
to the reader that this

01:37:31.910 --> 01:37:33.620 align:middle line:90%
is the main part of my program.

01:37:33.620 --> 01:37:38.030 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get rid of my empty Hello
call now and only pass in one version

01:37:38.030 --> 01:37:39.110 align:middle line:90%
with Hello, name.

01:37:39.110 --> 01:37:42.260 align:middle line:84%
And then down here, a couple
of lines further down,

01:37:42.260 --> 01:37:45.030 align:middle line:90%
I'll actually define my Hello function.

01:37:45.030 --> 01:37:47.870 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, now that I've
reordered the functions in this way,

01:37:47.870 --> 01:37:51.890 align:middle line:84%
by putting the main part of my code
at the top and Hello at the bottom

01:37:51.890 --> 01:37:55.310 align:middle line:84%
so that my logic kind of flows top
to bottom, if I go ahead and run

01:37:55.310 --> 01:37:57.680 align:middle line:90%
Python of Hello.py, Enter.

01:37:57.680 --> 01:38:00.290 align:middle line:90%
Nothing whatsoever happens.

01:38:00.290 --> 01:38:02.630 align:middle line:84%
If I do it again, nothing
whatsoever happens.

01:38:02.630 --> 01:38:04.520 align:middle line:90%
Well, why in the world is this?

01:38:04.520 --> 01:38:06.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, just because I've
defined a function called

01:38:06.740 --> 01:38:09.230 align:middle line:84%
Main and I've defined a
function called Hello,

01:38:09.230 --> 01:38:11.420 align:middle line:90%
doesn't mean that I've actually called--

01:38:11.420 --> 01:38:13.340 align:middle line:90%
that is used-- either of them.

01:38:13.340 --> 01:38:16.970 align:middle line:84%
Yes, I'm using Hello
inside of Main, but no one

01:38:16.970 --> 01:38:20.210 align:middle line:84%
is telling Python to
actually use or call Main.

01:38:20.210 --> 01:38:24.770 align:middle line:84%
So in order to tidy this up, the
last thing I need to do in this file,

01:38:24.770 --> 01:38:28.100 align:middle line:84%
it seems, is actually
call my main function.

01:38:28.100 --> 01:38:30.890 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, by calling my
main function in this way,

01:38:30.890 --> 01:38:33.890 align:middle line:84%
it gets me out of trouble because
now I'm defining Main first

01:38:33.890 --> 01:38:35.450 align:middle line:90%
but I'm not calling Hello yet.

01:38:35.450 --> 01:38:38.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm defining Hello next, but
I'm not calling Hello next.

01:38:38.760 --> 01:38:42.170 align:middle line:84%
I only at the very end
of this file call Main

01:38:42.170 --> 01:38:45.090 align:middle line:84%
which has the effect of
running this code up here,

01:38:45.090 --> 01:38:47.690 align:middle line:84%
which has the effect of
running this code down here,

01:38:47.690 --> 01:38:50.870 align:middle line:84%
and it allows me therefore
to organize my file

01:38:50.870 --> 01:38:55.520 align:middle line:84%
and order my functions in any way I
want, including Main at the very top,

01:38:55.520 --> 01:38:59.930 align:middle line:84%
and solving ultimately that problem
of Python not knowing what's going on.

01:38:59.930 --> 01:39:02.780 align:middle line:84%
Now it's important to note
that I defined my function

01:39:02.780 --> 01:39:05.690 align:middle line:84%
Hello as taking an
argument To and then I

01:39:05.690 --> 01:39:08.390 align:middle line:84%
passed into that function
the value of the variable

01:39:08.390 --> 01:39:11.570 align:middle line:84%
that I wanted to say Hello to,
that is the variable called Name.

01:39:11.570 --> 01:39:14.420 align:middle line:84%
Because suppose I had done
something a little bit differently.

01:39:14.420 --> 01:39:17.670 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that I hadn't defined
Hello is taking an argument,

01:39:17.670 --> 01:39:21.560 align:middle line:84%
so I just remove mention of To
and its default value "world."

01:39:21.560 --> 01:39:25.070 align:middle line:84%
And I go back up to my
main function and I just

01:39:25.070 --> 01:39:28.193 align:middle line:84%
call Hello itself without
passing in any arguments.

01:39:28.193 --> 01:39:30.360 align:middle line:84%
And now let me go ahead and
make my one more change,

01:39:30.360 --> 01:39:32.760 align:middle line:84%
one more mistake
technically, let me go ahead

01:39:32.760 --> 01:39:37.720 align:middle line:84%
and just try to naively print out the
value of Name in the Hello function.

01:39:37.720 --> 01:39:40.380 align:middle line:84%
So now to be clear, in my
main function on line 2,

01:39:40.380 --> 01:39:43.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm defining my variable called Name
and assigning it the return value

01:39:43.740 --> 01:39:45.480 align:middle line:90%
of the input function from the user.

01:39:45.480 --> 01:39:47.010 align:middle line:90%
I'm then just calling Hello.

01:39:47.010 --> 01:39:50.700 align:middle line:84%
In my Hello function, which now
no longer takes any arguments,

01:39:50.700 --> 01:39:54.180 align:middle line:84%
I am calling print,
passing in Hello comma,

01:39:54.180 --> 01:39:56.970 align:middle line:84%
and then immediately passing
in Name, the variable

01:39:56.970 --> 01:39:58.740 align:middle line:90%
into which I got the user's input.

01:39:58.740 --> 01:40:02.232 align:middle line:84%
But the catch is that name
exists now only in Main.

01:40:02.232 --> 01:40:05.190 align:middle line:84%
And so watch what happens when I try
to run this version of the program

01:40:05.190 --> 01:40:07.170 align:middle line:90%
with Python Hello.py.

01:40:07.170 --> 01:40:08.220 align:middle line:90%
I hit Enter.

01:40:08.220 --> 01:40:10.500 align:middle line:84%
I'm prompted for my
name, D-A-V-I-D, Enter.

01:40:10.500 --> 01:40:11.340 align:middle line:90%
And, argh!

01:40:11.340 --> 01:40:12.450 align:middle line:90%
A name error.

01:40:12.450 --> 01:40:15.400 align:middle line:90%
Name "Name" is not defined.

01:40:15.400 --> 01:40:19.050 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that this is actually
an issue of what's called scope.

01:40:19.050 --> 01:40:23.970 align:middle line:84%
Scope refers to a variable only existing
in the context in which you defined it.

01:40:23.970 --> 01:40:28.080 align:middle line:84%
So insofar as I define this
variable, Name in my main function,

01:40:28.080 --> 01:40:31.020 align:middle line:84%
I can only use that variable
in my name function.

01:40:31.020 --> 01:40:34.380 align:middle line:84%
I can't use it as I've tried
to here in my Hello function.

01:40:34.380 --> 01:40:36.930 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't exist in
that so-called scope.

01:40:36.930 --> 01:40:42.010 align:middle line:84%
And so this is why now, if I rewind
and undo all of those changes,

01:40:42.010 --> 01:40:45.690 align:middle line:84%
you'll see that I'm deliberately
passing Main from my main function

01:40:45.690 --> 01:40:47.340 align:middle line:90%
into my Hello function.

01:40:47.340 --> 01:40:50.260 align:middle line:84%
And now in the Hello function, it
technically has a different name.

01:40:50.260 --> 01:40:52.380 align:middle line:84%
It's called To in that
context, but that's fine.

01:40:52.380 --> 01:40:55.950 align:middle line:84%
It's completely up to each individual
function to name its own variables

01:40:55.950 --> 01:40:58.230 align:middle line:84%
or name its own arguments,
but this is a way now

01:40:58.230 --> 01:41:01.630 align:middle line:84%
that I'm handing to the Hello
function the value of that variable

01:41:01.630 --> 01:41:04.530 align:middle line:90%
so it can be printed by Hello as well.

01:41:04.530 --> 01:41:07.410 align:middle line:84%
And there's one final
flourish we can add here.

01:41:07.410 --> 01:41:10.590 align:middle line:84%
Now that we've implemented Hello,
you'll notice that Hello only

01:41:10.590 --> 01:41:12.120 align:middle line:90%
has a so-called side effect.

01:41:12.120 --> 01:41:14.580 align:middle line:84%
It only prints out
something to the screen.

01:41:14.580 --> 01:41:19.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, what if I also want my function
to not have a side effect, per se,

01:41:19.980 --> 01:41:22.300 align:middle line:90%
but actually hand me back a value?

01:41:22.300 --> 01:41:25.740 align:middle line:84%
Recall that the input
function returns a value,

01:41:25.740 --> 01:41:27.510 align:middle line:90%
the string that the user typed in.

01:41:27.510 --> 01:41:30.060 align:middle line:84%
Recall that the INT
function returns a value.

01:41:30.060 --> 01:41:33.960 align:middle line:84%
The float function returns a
value that was passed into it.

01:41:33.960 --> 01:41:37.260 align:middle line:84%
Well you can use one final
keyword here, literally

01:41:37.260 --> 01:41:40.890 align:middle line:84%
Return to return a value
explicitly yourself.

01:41:40.890 --> 01:41:43.890 align:middle line:84%
In fact, let me go back
to VS Code here, and I

01:41:43.890 --> 01:41:47.820 align:middle line:84%
think we'll return our
attention to Calculator.py

01:41:47.820 --> 01:41:53.190 align:middle line:84%
and see if we can't implement one other
version of Calculator.py that actually

01:41:53.190 --> 01:41:56.410 align:middle line:84%
has our own function that
even returns a value.

01:41:56.410 --> 01:41:59.800 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to go ahead
and open up calculator/py

01:41:59.800 --> 01:42:03.100 align:middle line:84%
and I think this time I'm going to
throw everything away as before,

01:42:03.100 --> 01:42:06.600 align:middle line:84%
and I'm just going to start
practicing what we're preaching here.

01:42:06.600 --> 01:42:09.120 align:middle line:84%
Define a function called
Main which is now going

01:42:09.120 --> 01:42:11.070 align:middle line:90%
to be the main part of my function.

01:42:11.070 --> 01:42:13.980 align:middle line:84%
Let's go ahead and now
declare a variable called x,

01:42:13.980 --> 01:42:19.350 align:middle line:84%
and assign it to the converted version
of the user's input after asking them,

01:42:19.350 --> 01:42:20.430 align:middle line:90%
what's x?

01:42:20.430 --> 01:42:23.490 align:middle line:84%
So again, a line of code
quite like we've done before.

01:42:23.490 --> 01:42:27.023 align:middle line:84%
And suppose now that what I
want to do is square this value.

01:42:27.023 --> 01:42:29.190 align:middle line:84%
I want to take the number
that the user has typed in

01:42:29.190 --> 01:42:30.840 align:middle line:90%
and raise it to the power of 2.

01:42:30.840 --> 01:42:32.790 align:middle line:90%
So 2 squared would be 4.

01:42:32.790 --> 01:42:34.410 align:middle line:90%
3 squared would be 9.

01:42:34.410 --> 01:42:36.840 align:middle line:90%
4 squared would be 16, and so forth.

01:42:36.840 --> 01:42:39.450 align:middle line:84%
Well how do I go about
implementing a function literally

01:42:39.450 --> 01:42:42.960 align:middle line:84%
called Square which actually
doesn't come with Python built-in?

01:42:42.960 --> 01:42:45.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me assume for the
moment that it does exist, and let

01:42:45.660 --> 01:42:47.530 align:middle line:90%
me say something like this--

01:42:47.530 --> 01:42:50.670 align:middle line:84%
let me go ahead and
say that printing, how

01:42:50.670 --> 01:42:57.720 align:middle line:90%
about x squared is comma square of x.

01:42:57.720 --> 01:42:59.170 align:middle line:90%
So what have I done?

01:42:59.170 --> 01:43:02.710 align:middle line:84%
I've defined a function called Main
and I've implemented two lines.

01:43:02.710 --> 01:43:05.190 align:middle line:84%
The first of these lines
prompts the user for a value

01:43:05.190 --> 01:43:09.330 align:middle line:84%
x and converts it to an INT and
stores it in a variable called x.

01:43:09.330 --> 01:43:12.930 align:middle line:84%
On line 3, I then say
x squared is and then

01:43:12.930 --> 01:43:16.620 align:middle line:84%
I pass a second argument to the
print function, whatever the return

01:43:16.620 --> 01:43:18.780 align:middle line:90%
value is of a square function.

01:43:18.780 --> 01:43:22.230 align:middle line:84%
But squared doesn't exist
and I'll show you this here,

01:43:22.230 --> 01:43:30.090 align:middle line:84%
if I now call Main at the bottom
and I run Python of Calculator.py,

01:43:30.090 --> 01:43:35.100 align:middle line:84%
I'll see that x is 2 and then I see a
whole bunch of errors, a name error,

01:43:35.100 --> 01:43:37.090 align:middle line:90%
name Square is not defined.

01:43:37.090 --> 01:43:39.810 align:middle line:84%
So this isn't a typo here, it's
just the function doesn't exist.

01:43:39.810 --> 01:43:41.790 align:middle line:90%
But I think I can make it exist here.

01:43:41.790 --> 01:43:45.180 align:middle line:84%
Let me go ahead and define
another function called Square.

01:43:45.180 --> 01:43:47.400 align:middle line:84%
This one's going to
take in a number and I'm

01:43:47.400 --> 01:43:50.370 align:middle line:84%
going to call it generically
n, as many a programmer would,

01:43:50.370 --> 01:43:52.260 align:middle line:90%
just to represent any old number.

01:43:52.260 --> 01:43:56.010 align:middle line:84%
And then what do I want to
do in order to square n?

01:43:56.010 --> 01:43:59.380 align:middle line:84%
Well a number squared is really
just itself times itself,

01:43:59.380 --> 01:44:00.720 align:middle line:90%
so I'm going to do this--

01:44:00.720 --> 01:44:02.340 align:middle line:90%
n times n.

01:44:02.340 --> 01:44:06.180 align:middle line:84%
But it's not enough just to do
the math yourself, n times n.

01:44:06.180 --> 01:44:09.990 align:middle line:84%
You're going to have to return
the actual value n times n

01:44:09.990 --> 01:44:11.970 align:middle line:90%
and that's our new keyword here.

01:44:11.970 --> 01:44:14.100 align:middle line:90%
When I now do this, watch what happens.

01:44:14.100 --> 01:44:16.230 align:middle line:90%
Python of Calculator.py, Enter.

01:44:16.230 --> 01:44:20.250 align:middle line:90%
X say shall be 2, x squared is 4.

01:44:20.250 --> 01:44:24.020 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead now and say x is now 3.

01:44:24.020 --> 01:44:26.010 align:middle line:90%
X squared is now 9.

01:44:26.010 --> 01:44:30.060 align:middle line:84%
So I've implemented my very own function
that returns the square of a value

01:44:30.060 --> 01:44:33.180 align:middle line:84%
and because I'm using
the return keyword, that

01:44:33.180 --> 01:44:36.120 align:middle line:84%
ensures that I can pass the
return value of this, just

01:44:36.120 --> 01:44:40.140 align:middle line:84%
like the return value of input or
INT or float, to another function,

01:44:40.140 --> 01:44:41.460 align:middle line:90%
like print instead.

01:44:41.460 --> 01:44:44.520 align:middle line:84%
And here too there's going to be so
many ways to solve this same problem.

01:44:44.520 --> 01:44:47.490 align:middle line:84%
I can actually raise
n to the power of 2.

01:44:47.490 --> 01:44:49.620 align:middle line:84%
We've not seen this
syntax before, but if you

01:44:49.620 --> 01:44:54.220 align:middle line:84%
use two asterisks like this, two stars,
that raises the thing on the left

01:44:54.220 --> 01:44:55.590 align:middle line:90%
to the power on the right.

01:44:55.590 --> 01:44:58.290 align:middle line:84%
Or it turns out there is
in Python a function called

01:44:58.290 --> 01:45:00.480 align:middle line:84%
POW for raising something
to the power that

01:45:00.480 --> 01:45:04.200 align:middle line:84%
takes two arguments, the first of which
is the number, the second of which

01:45:04.200 --> 01:45:05.140 align:middle line:90%
is the exponent.

01:45:05.140 --> 01:45:09.810 align:middle line:84%
So there too, there's just so many ways
to actually solve that same problem

01:45:09.810 --> 01:45:11.290 align:middle line:90%
as well.

01:45:11.290 --> 01:45:13.020 align:middle line:90%
So ultimately, what have we done here?

01:45:13.020 --> 01:45:15.960 align:middle line:84%
We first introduced functions--
these actions or verbs, many of which

01:45:15.960 --> 01:45:19.000 align:middle line:84%
come built into Python that you
can just use in your own code.

01:45:19.000 --> 01:45:22.290 align:middle line:84%
We then introduced variables via which
you could store those return values

01:45:22.290 --> 01:45:24.120 align:middle line:84%
and then maybe do
something more with it.

01:45:24.120 --> 01:45:25.350 align:middle line:90%
At the end of the day too.

01:45:25.350 --> 01:45:28.260 align:middle line:84%
You now have the ability to create,
to invent your own functions

01:45:28.260 --> 01:45:30.750 align:middle line:84%
to solve simple problems
like Hello, or in the weeks

01:45:30.750 --> 01:45:36.590 align:middle line:84%
to come-- much more sophisticated, more
challenging, more fun problems as well.

01:45:36.590 --> 01:45:38.000 align:middle line:90%